{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018PKH7JasSnGk8tffqBGock","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: 2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched device upload per round. Each MLE round iterates over ~310 traces (at APC 300) and calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` individually, issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` for a tiny buffer (~16-48 bytes) per trace. The plan estimated ~3000-4000 unnecessary allocation+copy+free triplets across all rounds and segments, each also acquiring the global `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex. Consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading once was expected to save 20-40ms from the ~170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t### Files changed\n10\t\n11\t- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`**: Changed `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Updated all consumers (`ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`, `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`, `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`, `evaluate_single_logup`) to use `t.main_ptrs_ptr` instead of `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`.\n12\t\n13\t- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`**: Updated `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new`, `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new`, `LogupMonomialBatch::new` to use `t.main_ptrs_ptr` instead of `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`. Updated doc comment referencing `main_ptrs_dev`.\n14\t\n15\t- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`**: Changed `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu` and `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu` parameter from `&DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `*const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`, removing the `.as_ptr()` indirection inside.\n16\t\n17\t- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`**: In `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`:\n18\t  - **Case A (late_eval)**: Collect all main_ptrs into `all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets during the loop. After the loop, do a single `to_device()` and backfill pointers into each `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_ptr`. Keep the `DeviceBuffer` alive via `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs`.\n19\t  - **Case B (early_eval)**: Same pattern — collect into `all_early_main_ptrs` with offsets, single `to_device()` after Phase 3 loop, backfill pointers, keep alive via `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n20\t\n21\t- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs`**: Updated `test_monomial_vs_dag_equivalence` to use `main_ptrs_ptr: _main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` and keep the `DeviceBuffer` alive via `_main_ptrs_dev`.\n22\t\n23\t### Key decisions\n24\t\n25\t- Used the backfill approach: push TraceCtx with `main_ptrs_ptr: null()`, then overwrite after the batch upload. This avoids restructuring the existing loop logic.\n26\t- No deviations from the plan.\n27\t\n28\t## Results\n29\t\n30\t### APC 300\n31\t\n32\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n33\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n34\t| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1417ms | 1421ms | -1034ms, 1.73x lower | +4ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n35\t| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 170ms | 165ms | -15ms, 1.09x lower | -5ms, 1.03x lower |\n36\t| Round 0 | 662ms | 295ms | 296ms | -366ms, 2.24x lower | +1ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n37\t| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 518ms | 533ms | -257ms, 1.48x lower | +15ms (noise) |\n38\t\n39\t### APC 100\n40\t\n41\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n42\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n43\t| STARK excl trace | 2155ms | 1583ms | 1593ms | -562ms, 1.35x lower | +10ms, 1.01x (noise) |\n44\t| MLE Rounds | 150ms | 140ms | 135ms | -15ms, 1.11x lower | -5ms, 1.04x lower |\n45\t\n46\t### APC 0\n47\t\n48\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n49\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n50\t| STARK excl trace | 2153ms | 2147ms | 2150ms | -3ms, 1.00x | +3ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n51\t| MLE Rounds | 118ms | 114ms | 113ms | -5ms, 1.04x lower | -1ms, 1.01x lower |\n52\t\n53\t### CUDA API call counts (from nsys)\n54\t\n55\t| Metric | Before | After | Delta |\n56\t|--------|--------|-------|-------|\n57\t| H2D copies | 30,035 | 24,436 | -5,599 |\n58\t\n59\t## Assessment\n60\t\n61\t**The optimization did NOT meet its success criteria.** MLE Rounds at APC 300 improved by only 5ms (170ms → 165ms), well below the plan's 10ms rollback threshold. STARK excl trace showed no net improvement (+4ms, within noise).\n62\t\n63\tThe nsys data confirms the batching is mechanically correct: ~5,600 fewer H2D copy calls. However, the per-call overhead of these small uploads was much lower than estimated:\n64\t- **Estimated**: ~15-20μs per call (mutex + cudaMallocAsync + cudaMemcpyAsync + cudaFreeAsync + HashMap ops)\n65\t- **Actual**: ~1μs per call (5ms improvement / 5600 eliminated calls ≈ 0.9μs each)\n66\t\n67\tThe discrepancy is likely because:\n68\t1. `cudaMallocAsync` with small allocations may be served from a pool cache, avoiding actual allocation\n69\t2. The MEMORY_MANAGER mutex is uncontended in the single-threaded MLE rounds path (unlike multi-stream Round 0/GKR)\n70\t3. `cudaMemcpyAsync` for 16-48 bytes is essentially free on the PCIe bus\n71\t\n72\tNo regression at APC 0 (MLE Rounds -1ms, STARK excl trace +3ms, both noise).\n73\t\n74\tPer rollback criterion #1 (MLE Rounds improvement < 10ms), this optimization is **reverted**.\n75\t\n76\t## Future Work\n77\t\n78\t- **What worked**: The batching pattern is sound and the code change is minimal/clean. The `_keepalive` pattern for shared device buffers with raw pointer offsets is reusable.\n79\t- **Root cause of small impact**: The MLE rounds path is single-threaded, so mutex contention is near-zero. The dominant MLE Rounds cost is GPU kernel execution, not CUDA API overhead.\n80\t- **More impactful MLE Rounds targets**: The remaining 165ms is dominated by actual kernel execution time across ~16 invocations of `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`. Further improvement would require:\n81\t  - Reducing the number of MLE rounds (algorithmic change)\n82\t  - Making the per-round GPU kernels faster (kernel optimization)\n83\t  - Batching the evaluation kernels themselves across traces (not just the upload)\n84\t- **Potential combination**: This optimization could be combined with multi-stream MLE rounds if that approach is pursued, where mutex contention would be higher and the per-call overhead would increase.\n85\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"e3cf0ff4-24dd-45dc-bea5-8f13fb347322","timestamp":"2026-04-13T10:02:16.665Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload/report.md","content":"# Report: 2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\n\n## Description\n\nReplace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched device upload per round. Each MLE round iterates over ~310 traces (at APC 300) and calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` individually, issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` for a tiny buffer (~16-48 bytes) per trace. The plan estimated ~3000-4000 unnecessary allocation+copy+free triplets across all rounds and segments, each also acquiring the global `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex. Consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading once was expected to save 20-40ms from the ~170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Files changed\n\n- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`**: Changed `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Updated all consumers (`ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new`, `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new`, `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`, `evaluate_single_logup`) to use `t.main_ptrs_ptr` instead of `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`.\n\n- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs`**: Updated `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new`, `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new`, `LogupMonomialBatch::new` to use `t.main_ptrs_ptr` instead of `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`. Updated doc comment referencing `main_ptrs_dev`.\n\n- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`**: Changed `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu` and `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu` parameter from `&DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `*const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`, removing the `.as_ptr()` indirection inside.\n\n- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`**: In `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`:\n  - **Case A (late_eval)**: Collect all main_ptrs into `all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets during the loop. After the loop, do a single `to_device()` and backfill pointers into each `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_ptr`. Keep the `DeviceBuffer` alive via `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs`.\n  - **Case B (early_eval)**: Same pattern — collect into `all_early_main_ptrs` with offsets, single `to_device()` after Phase 3 loop, backfill pointers, keep alive via `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n\n- **`crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs`**: Updated `test_monomial_vs_dag_equivalence` to use `main_ptrs_ptr: _main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` and keep the `DeviceBuffer` alive via `_main_ptrs_dev`.\n\n### Key decisions\n\n- Used the backfill approach: push TraceCtx with `main_ptrs_ptr: null()`, then overwrite after the batch upload. This avoids restructuring the existing loop logic.\n- No deviations from the plan.\n\n## Results\n\n### APC 300\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1417ms | 1421ms | -1034ms, 1.73x lower | +4ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 170ms | 165ms | -15ms, 1.09x lower | -5ms, 1.03x lower |\n| Round 0 | 662ms | 295ms | 296ms | -366ms, 2.24x lower | +1ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 518ms | 533ms | -257ms, 1.48x lower | +15ms (noise) |\n\n### APC 100\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2155ms | 1583ms | 1593ms | -562ms, 1.35x lower | +10ms, 1.01x (noise) |\n| MLE Rounds | 150ms | 140ms | 135ms | -15ms, 1.11x lower | -5ms, 1.04x lower |\n\n### APC 0\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| STARK excl trace | 2153ms | 2147ms | 2150ms | -3ms, 1.00x | +3ms, 1.00x (noise) |\n| MLE Rounds | 118ms | 114ms | 113ms | -5ms, 1.04x lower | -1ms, 1.01x lower |\n\n### CUDA API call counts (from nsys)\n\n| Metric | Before | After | Delta |\n|--------|--------|-------|-------|\n| H2D copies | 30,035 | 24,436 | -5,599 |\n\n## Assessment\n\n**The optimization did NOT meet its success criteria.** MLE Rounds at APC 300 improved by only 5ms (170ms → 165ms), well below the plan's 10ms rollback threshold. STARK excl trace showed no net improvement (+4ms, within noise).\n\nThe nsys data confirms the batching is mechanically correct: ~5,600 fewer H2D copy calls. However, the per-call overhead of these small uploads was much lower than estimated:\n- **Estimated**: ~15-20μs per call (mutex + cudaMallocAsync + cudaMemcpyAsync + cudaFreeAsync + HashMap ops)\n- **Actual**: ~1μs per call (5ms improvement / 5600 eliminated calls ≈ 0.9μs each)\n\nThe discrepancy is likely because:\n1. `cudaMallocAsync` with small allocations may be served from a pool cache, avoiding actual allocation\n2. The MEMORY_MANAGER mutex is uncontended in the single-threaded MLE rounds path (unlike multi-stream Round 0/GKR)\n3. `cudaMemcpyAsync` for 16-48 bytes is essentially free on the PCIe bus\n\nNo regression at APC 0 (MLE Rounds -1ms, STARK excl trace +3ms, both noise).\n\nPer rollback criterion #1 (MLE Rounds improvement < 10ms), this optimization is **reverted**.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **What worked**: The batching pattern is sound and the code change is minimal/clean. The `_keepalive` pattern for shared device buffers with raw pointer offsets is reusable.\n- **Root cause of small impact**: The MLE rounds path is single-threaded, so mutex contention is near-zero. The dominant MLE Rounds cost is GPU kernel execution, not CUDA API overhead.\n- **More impactful MLE Rounds targets**: The remaining 165ms is dominated by actual kernel execution time across ~16 invocations of `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval`. Further improvement would require:\n  - Reducing the number of MLE rounds (algorithmic change)\n  - Making the per-round GPU kernels faster (kernel optimization)\n  - Batching the evaluation kernels themselves across traces (not just the upload)\n- **Potential combination**: This optimization could be combined with multi-stream MLE rounds if that approach is pursued, where mutex contention would be higher and the per-call overhead would increase.\n","numLines":85,"startLine":1,"totalLines":85}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XsRAyndJf4JNgYs71hWYad","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I've read all the context files and reports. Let me now read the most recent reports to understand the state better. Let me check what the current state of the codebase is. I need to understand what has been tried and what the current performance looks like.\n\nLet me summarize what I know:\n\n**Baseline (APC 300)**: STARK excl trace = 2455ms\n**Current best (after successful optimizations)**: STARK excl trace at APC 300 = ~1418ms (1.73x lower vs baseline)\n\n**Successful optimizations so far:**\n1. Batch stacked reduction MLE sync: SR 311ms → 126ms (2.47x)\n\n\n2. Multistream Round 0: Round 0 662ms → 351ms (1.89x)\n3. Multistream GKR input eval: GKR 790ms → 564ms (1.40x)\n4. Batch stacking scatter kernel: Trace Commit 406ms → 255ms (1.59x)\n5. Increase thread count 4→8: -61ms overall\n6. Prealloc GKR input buffers: GKR eval 532ms → 318ms (1.67x)\n7. Batch MLE round kernels: SR 123ms → 75ms (4.15x vs baseline)\n8. Batch MLE interpolation: M\n\nNow looking at the MLE rounds and STARK trace completion times. A few optimizations didn't pan out—the pipeline kernel launches and Round 0 buffer preallocation actually hurt performance due to memory contention, while some other tweaks only yielded marginal gains. Breaking down the current STARK execution at APC 300, I'm seeing the trace exclusion time at around 1418ms, which is a solid 1.73x improvement from the baseline. The major components are Round 0 at 295ms, LogUp GKR consuming 518-533ms, MLE rounds at 165-168ms, stacked reduction at 74-75ms, trace commit at 250ms, and openings likely taking another 200-300ms.\n\nLet me cross-check this against the baseline spec output to make sure the numbers align properly. Now I'm reconciling the numbers from the batch MLE interpolation report. The Stacked Reduction component dropped significantly from 311ms 