ound 0 does for each AIR instance and also check what's happening with MLE Rounds at APC 300.\n\nLooking at the bigger picture, I'm trying to hit a 2x speedup target which means getting STARK execution down to 1078ms from the current 1409ms at APC 300. The main evaluation kernels are still launching per-AIR rather than being batched, so that's where the biggest gains likely are. LogUp GKR and Trace Commit are already scaling well, so the real wins are in Round 0 and MLE Rounds—together they could save 333ms and get us close to target. The approach would be batching Round 0 across all AIRs using a descriptor-array strategy similar to what worked for stacked reduction, which means restructuring how constraints and interactions are evaluated in a single kernel launch.\n\nFor MLE Rounds, I'd apply the same batching pattern to the zerocheck and logup evaluation kernels, though those are more complex. But before diving into implementation, I should actually profile with nsys to see where the time is really going within these components, and review the source code for Round 0 and MLE paths to figure out which approach is most feasible.\n\nLooking at the numbers more carefully now—at APC 300, Round 0 takes 294ms after the multi-stream optimization across 8 threads. I'm realizing there's some confusion about how the batching works. The prover groups AIRs into memory batches of 20-42 each based on memory budget constraints, and then the multi-stream optimization distributes those AIRs across threads. So at APC 300 with roughly 310 AIRs per segment, they're split into these memory-constrained batches and processed in parallel.\n\nBut I'm noticing the threshold logic doesn't quite add up—if individual batches are under 100 AIRs, multi-threading shouldn't trigger, yet the optimization clearly delivered a 2.25x speedup. I need to look at the actual implementation to understand what threshold is being applied and where. Let me profile with nsys on APC 300 to see where the time is 