{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JxuMTyUbDRVtjmjCAXrRDT","type":"tool_result","content":"140\tfn process_air_round0<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n141\t    w: &Round0AirWorkItem<HS>,\n142\t) -> Result<Round0AirResult, LogupZerocheckError> {\n143\t    let single_pk = w.single_pk;\n144\t    let single_air_constraints = SymbolicConstraints::from(&single_pk.vk.symbolic_constraints);\n145\t    let local_constraint_deg = single_pk.vk.max_constraint_degree as usize;\n146\t    assert!(\n147\t        local_constraint_deg <= w.constraint_degree,\n148\t        \"Max constraint degree ({local_constraint_deg}) of AIR exceeds the global maximum {}\",\n149\t        w.constraint_degree\n150\t    );\n151\t\n152\t    let log_large_domain = log2_ceil_usize(local_constraint_deg << w.l_skip);\n153\t    let omega_root = F::two_adic_generator(log_large_domain);\n154\t    assert!(!w.xi.is_empty(), \"xi vector must not be empty\");\n155\t\n156\t    let height = w.common_main.height();\n157\t    let mut main_parts = Vec::with_capacity(w.cached_mains.len() + 1);\n158\t    for committed in w.cached_mains {\n159\t        main_parts.push(committed.trace.buffer().as_ptr());\n160\t    }\n161\t    main_parts.push(w.common_main.buffer().as_ptr());\n162\t    let d_main_parts = main_parts.to_device()?;\n163\t\n164\t    let n_lift = w.n.max(0) as usize;\n165\t    let eq_xi_tree = &w.eq_xis[&n_lift];\n166\t    let num_cosets_zc = local_constraint_deg.saturating_sub(1);\n167\t\n168\t    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(\n169\t        single_pk,\n170\t        w.selectors_cube.buffer(),\n171\t        &d_main_parts,\n172\t        w.public_values,\n173\t        eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(n_lift),\n174\t        w.d_lambda_pows,\n175\t        1 << w.l_skip,\n176\t        1 << n_lift,\n177\t        height as u32,\n178\t        num_cosets_zc as u32,\n179\t        omega_root,\n180\t        w.max_temp_bytes,\n181\t    )?;\n182\t\n183\t    let zerocheck_poly = if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n184\t        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host_on_current_stream()?;\n185\t        let q = {\n186\t            let mut values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_zc << w.l_skip);\n187\t            for coset_idx in 0..num_cosets_zc {\n188\t                for i in 0..1 << w.l_skip {\n189\t                    values[i * num_cosets_zc + coset_idx] = q_evals[(coset_idx << w.l_skip) + i];\n190\t                }\n191\t            }\n192\t            UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n193\t                RowMajorMatrix::new(values, num_cosets_zc),\n194\t                omega_root,\n195\t                omega_root,\n196\t            )\n197\t        };\n198\t        let sp_0_deg = sumcheck_round0_deg(w.l_skip, local_constraint_deg);\n199\t        let coeffs = (0..=sp_0_deg)\n200\t            .map(|i| {\n201\t                let mut c = -*q.coeffs().get(i).unwrap_or(&EF::ZERO);\n202\t                if i >= 1 << w.l_skip {\n203\t                    c += q.coeffs()[i - (1 << w.l_skip)];\n204\t                }\n205\t                c\n206\t            })\n207\t            .collect_vec();\n208\t        Some(UnivariatePoly::new(coeffs))\n209\t    } else {\n210\t        None\n211\t    };\n212\t\n213\t    let num_cosets_logup = local_constraint_deg;\n214\t    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(\n215\t        single_pk,\n216\t        &single_air_constraints,\n217\t        w.selectors_cube.buffer(),\n218\t        &d_main_parts,\n219\t        w.public_values,\n220\t        eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(n_lift),\n221\t        w.beta_pows,\n222\t        w.eq_3bs,\n223\t        1 << w.l_skip,\n224\t        1 << n_lift,\n225\t        height as u32,\n226\t        num_cosets_logup as u32,\n227\t        omega_root,\n228\t        w.max_temp_bytes,\n229\t    )?;\n230\t\n231\t    let (logup_numer_poly, logup_denom_poly) = if !sum.is_empty() {\n232\t        let evals = sum.to_host_on_current_stream()?;\n233\t        let (mut numer, denom): (Vec<EF>, Vec<EF>) =\n234\t            evals.into_iter().map(|frac| (frac.p, frac.q)).unzip();\n235\t        if w.n.is_negative() {\n236\t            let norm_factor = F::from_u32(1 << w.n.unsigned_abs()).inverse();\n237\t            for s in &mut numer {\n238\t                *s *= norm_factor;\n239\t            }\n240\t        }\n241\t        let mut numer_values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_logup << w.l_skip);\n242\t        let mut denom_values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_logup << w.l_skip);\n243\t        for coset_idx in 0..num_cosets_logup {\n244\t            for i in 0..1 << w.l_skip {\n245\t                let src = (coset_idx << w.l_skip) + i;\n246\t                let dst = i * num_cosets_logup + coset_idx;\n247\t                numer_values[dst] = numer[src];\n248\t                denom_values[dst] = denom[src];\n249\t            }\n250\t        }\n251\t        let numer_poly = UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n252\t            RowMajorMatrix::new(numer_values, num_cosets_logup),\n253\t            omega_root,\n254\t            F::ONE,\n255\t        );\n256\t        let denom_poly = UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n257\t            RowMajorMatrix::new(denom_values, num_cosets_logup),\n258\t            omega_root,\n259\t            F::ONE,\n260\t        );\n261\t        (Some(numer_poly), Some(denom_poly))\n262\t    } else {\n263\t        (None, None)\n264\t    };\n265\t\n266\t    Ok(Round0AirResult {\n267\t        trace_idx: w.trace_idx,\n268\t        zerocheck_poly,\n269\t        logup_numer_poly,\n270\t        logup_denom_poly,\n271\t    })\n272\t}\n273\t\n274\t#[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]\n275\t#[instrument(level = \"info\", skip_all)]\n276\tpub fn prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu<HS, TS>(\n277\t    transcript: &mut TS,\n278\t    mpk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n279\t    ctx: &ProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"026ac96b-f600-4e46-a111-f7d4c6c318a3","timestamp":"2026-04-13T10:13:26.940Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs","content":"fn process_air_round0<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    w: &Round0AirWorkItem<HS>,\n) -> Result<Round0AirResult, LogupZerocheckError> {\n    let single_pk = w.single_pk;\n    let single_air_constraints = SymbolicConstraints::from(&single_pk.vk.symbolic_constraints);\n    let local_constraint_deg = single_pk.vk.max_constraint_degree as usize;\n    assert!(\n        local_constraint_deg <= w.constraint_degree,\n        \"Max constraint degree ({local_constraint_deg}) of AIR exceeds the global maximum {}\",\n        w.constraint_degree\n    );\n\n    let log_large_domain = log2_ceil_usize(local_constraint_deg << w.l_skip);\n    let omega_root = F::two_adic_generator(log_large_domain);\n    assert!(!w.xi.is_empty(), \"xi vector must not be empty\");\n\n    let height = w.common_main.height();\n    let mut main_parts = Vec::with_capacity(w.cached_mains.len() + 1);\n    for committed in w.cached_mains {\n        main_parts.push(committed.trace.buffer().as_ptr());\n    }\n    main_parts.push(w.common_main.buffer().as_ptr());\n    let d_main_parts = main_parts.to_device()?;\n\n    let n_lift = w.n.max(0) as usize;\n    let eq_xi_tree = &w.eq_xis[&n_lift];\n    let num_cosets_zc = local_constraint_deg.saturating_sub(1);\n\n    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(\n        single_pk,\n        w.selectors_cube.buffer(),\n        &d_main_parts,\n        w.public_values,\n        eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(n_lift),\n        w.d_lambda_pows,\n        1 << w.l_skip,\n        1 << n_lift,\n        height as u32,\n        num_cosets_zc as u32,\n        omega_root,\n        w.max_temp_bytes,\n    )?;\n\n    let zerocheck_poly = if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host_on_current_stream()?;\n        let q = {\n            let mut values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_zc << w.l_skip);\n            for coset_idx in 0..num_cosets_zc {\n                for i in 0..1 << w.l_skip {\n                    values[i * num_cosets_zc + coset_idx] = q_evals[(coset_idx << w.l_skip) + i];\n                }\n            }\n            UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n                RowMajorMatrix::new(values, num_cosets_zc),\n                omega_root,\n                omega_root,\n            )\n        };\n        let sp_0_deg = sumcheck_round0_deg(w.l_skip, local_constraint_deg);\n        let coeffs = (0..=sp_0_deg)\n            .map(|i| {\n                let mut c = -*q.coeffs().get(i).unwrap_or(&EF::ZERO);\n                if i >= 1 << w.l_skip {\n                    c += q.coeffs()[i - (1 << w.l_skip)];\n                }\n                c\n            })\n            .collect_vec();\n        Some(UnivariatePoly::new(coeffs))\n    } else {\n        None\n    };\n\n    let num_cosets_logup = local_constraint_deg;\n    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(\n        single_pk,\n        &single_air_constraints,\n        w.selectors_cube.buffer(),\n        &d_main_parts,\n        w.public_values,\n        eq_xi_tree.get_ptr(n_lift),\n        w.beta_pows,\n        w.eq_3bs,\n        1 << w.l_skip,\n        1 << n_lift,\n        height as u32,\n        num_cosets_logup as u32,\n        omega_root,\n        w.max_temp_bytes,\n    )?;\n\n    let (logup_numer_poly, logup_denom_poly) = if !sum.is_empty() {\n        let evals = sum.to_host_on_current_stream()?;\n        let (mut numer, denom): (Vec<EF>, Vec<EF>) =\n            evals.into_iter().map(|frac| (frac.p, frac.q)).unzip();\n        if w.n.is_negative() {\n            let norm_factor = F::from_u32(1 << w.n.unsigned_abs()).inverse();\n            for s in &mut numer {\n                *s *= norm_factor;\n            }\n        }\n        let mut numer_values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_logup << w.l_skip);\n        let mut denom_values = EF::zero_vec(num_cosets_logup << w.l_skip);\n        for coset_idx in 0..num_cosets_logup {\n            for i in 0..1 << w.l_skip {\n                let src = (coset_idx << w.l_skip) + i;\n                let dst = i * num_cosets_logup + coset_idx;\n                numer_values[dst] = numer[src];\n                denom_values[dst] = denom[src];\n            }\n        }\n        let numer_poly = UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n            RowMajorMatrix::new(numer_values, num_cosets_logup),\n            omega_root,\n            F::ONE,\n        );\n        let denom_poly = UnivariatePoly::from_geometric_cosets_evals_idft(\n            RowMajorMatrix::new(denom_values, num_cosets_logup),\n            omega_root,\n            F::ONE,\n        );\n        (Some(numer_poly), Some(denom_poly))\n    } else {\n        (None, None)\n    };\n\n    Ok(Round0AirResult {\n        trace_idx: w.trace_idx,\n        zerocheck_poly,\n        logup_numer_poly,\n        logup_denom_poly,\n    })\n}\n\n#[allow(clippy::type_complexity)]\n#[instrument(level = \"info\", skip_all)]\npub fn prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu<HS, TS>(\n    transcript: &mut TS,\n    mpk: &DeviceMultiStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,\n    ctx: &ProvingContext<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,","numLines":140,"startLine":140,"totalLines":1772}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017apGERGscAUsE6tpk6VFwn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me analyze the per-AIR work in `process_air_round0`:\n\n1. **SymbolicConstraints::from** (line 144): Constructs symbolic constraints from the proving key. This is a CPU-only operation.\n2. **main_parts construction** (lines 157-162): Build a vector of pointers, upload to device. 1 H2D copy per AIR.\n3. **evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu** (line 168): Launches 1 kernel + 1 reduction kernel. Allocates intermediates + temp_sums + output buffers.\n\n\n4. **D2H copy** (line 184): Results come back to the host.\n5. **CPU post-processing** (lines 185-208): Transpose, iDFT, polynomial construction on the CPU side.\n6. **evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu** (line 214): Constructs the interaction DAG and launches 1 kernel + 1 reduction. Allocates intermediates + temp_sums + output + rules + weights buffers.\n7. **D2H copy** (line 232): Brings results back to host.\n8. **CPU post-processing** (lines 233-261): Unzips and processes the interaction results.\n\nLooking at the GPU operations per round, there are 2 H2D copies for the main parts and interaction rules/weights, 2 kernel launches for constraint evaluation with reduction, 2 more for interaction evaluation with reduction, 2 D2H copies for results, and 6-8 buffer allocations. At APC 300 with ~310 AIRs per segment, this scales to roughly 620 H2D copies, 1240 kernel launches, 620 D2H copies, and over 2000 buffer allocations—a massive amount of overhead. The CPU post-processing involves transpose and iDFT operations, which become a bottleneck at larger APC values.\n\nA previous attempt to parallelize the CPU post-processing only yielded 10ms improvement, confirming that GPU overhead is the real problem. The core issue is that `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` constructs a new DAG for each AIR on the CPU, which includes building a symbolic DAG builder, adding expressions from symbolic interactions, creating GPU rules from the DAG, computing weights from challenges, and encoding the rules.\n\nSince steps 1-3 are deterministic and depend only on the AIR's static symbolic constraints, I can pre-compute and cache the DAG structure and encoded rules at keygen time. Only the weight computation in step 4 depends on challenge values. My strategy is to batch Round 0 kernel launches using a descriptor-array pattern (like the MLE batch kernels) combined with this DAG caching, which would eliminate over 2000 GPU buffer allocations per segment, reduce 1240 kernel launches down to 4, cut 620 H2D and D2H copies to just 2 each, and eliminate the CPU descriptor array overhead entirely.\n\nLooking at the expected impact, Round 0 at APC 300 should drop from 294ms to around 100-120ms (a 2.5-3x speedup), bringing the overall STARK time down to roughly 1215-1235ms. But this is a substantial refactor, so I'm reconsidering whether to start with something simpler—maybe batching just the zerocheck constraint evaluation kernel first, since it doesn't require DAG construction. Though actually, the zerocheck constraints also use a DAG (`d_rules` in the kernel), so that approach might not be simpler after all.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the key difference: the zerocheck constraint DAG is pre-computed and cached in the proving key via `pk.other_data.rules`, whereas the interaction DAG gets constructed fresh at prove time for each AIR. So zerocheck batching is actually much more straightforward—I just need buffer allocation and kernel launches per AIR since all the metadata is already there. The interaction evaluation is the harder part because it either needs pre-computed DAGs at keygen or a batch descriptor that tracks per-AIR interaction DAG info. I should focus the plan on batching zerocheck constraints first, with interaction evaluation as a potential follow-up.\n\nLooking at the profiling data, the constraint evaluation kernel fires 539 times with 238ms total, while interaction evaluation is similarly expensive at 277ms across multiple kernels. Batching just constraints would cut the overhead roughly in half, but interaction would still have per-AIR overhead. If I batch both, the savings are much more substantial, but that requires caching the interaction DAG at keygen time. I need to weigh whether the extra complexity of DAG caching is worth the overhead reduction.\n\nExamining how the interaction rules are built, they're derived from a symbolic expression DAG that gets constructed and compiled into GPU rules during proving. The question is whether I can precompute and cache this DAG structure at keygen instead of rebuilding it for each proof.\n\nThe key insight is that the rules themselves are static—they depend only on the symbolic interactions which are part of the proving key. What changes between proofs are the weights, which are computed as linear combinations of dynamic values like `eq_3bs` and `beta_pows`. So I could cache the encoded rules and the DAG structure at keygen, then at prove time just recompute the weights using the cached mappings and the new dynamic values.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the batching strategy more carefully. The CPU post-processing in Round 0 only saves about 10ms even when parallelized, but in a batched approach I could overlap it with GPU work on the next batch. More importantly, I need to understand the actual bottleneck—how much time is spent on D2H transfers versus the GPU kernel itself, since batching changes when and how those transfers happen.\n\nLooking at the data transfer sizes, each AIR produces relatively small outputs—just a few thousand elements across all 310 AIRs at APC 300, which amounts to only about 160KB total. That's negligible for a D2H copy, so the transfer overhead isn't the limiting factor here. This gives me confidence that the batching approach is viable and I can now move forward with formulating concrete optimization ideas.\n\nThe key insight is that Round 0 spends three-quarters of its time on overhead—buffer allocation, kernel launches, and data movement—while only a quarter goes to actual computation. Batching multiple constraint evaluations into a single kernel with descriptor arrays would eliminate most of that overhead. I'm also considering whether to batch the interaction evaluation alongside it, and whether caching the interaction DAG structure at keygen time would help reduce redundant work.\n\nBeyond Round 0, I could apply similar multi-stream parallelism to the MLE Rounds, which currently run single-threaded. And there's potential to reduce CUDA API overhead by replacing the global memory manager mutex with per-thread stream-ordered memory pools, which would let each thread manage its own allocations without contention.\n\nI'm also considering fusing the fill_zero operation directly into the stack_columns_kernel during Trace Commit to eliminate a separate pass. Now I'm ranking these ideas by their impact-to-complexity ratio to see which ones are worth pursuing first.\n\nLooking at the scores, Idea 1 (constraint-only Round 0 batching) actually comes out ahead at 53.1, followed by Idea 2 (full Round 0 batching) at 40.8. But I need to think about absolute impact here—even the best option only gets us to around 1.79x scaling, which falls short of the 2x target. I need to find a way to combine multiple optimizations or discover something with even larger gains.\n\nIdea 1 is appealing as a stepping stone since batching constraint evaluation is simpler than the full interaction kernel. The MLE batch kernels task showed this approach works, and Round 0 follows a similar pattern, just with more complexity. But there's a critical difference I'm noticing: in the MLE batching, all AIRs shared the same `num_x` within a round, whereas Round 0 has different AIRs with different `num_x` values based on their lift parameters. This is a fundamental structural difference that could complicate the batching strategy.\n\nHowever, I could handle this the same way the MLE batching does—group AIRs by their `num_x` value and batch within each group. The question is how many distinct `num_x` values actually appear at APC 300, which depends on the distribution of `n_lift` across the AIRs.\n\nLooking at the actual data, there are probably around 5-10 distinct `num_x` values, with some groups having many AIRs and others just a few. This would reduce the kernel launches from ~310 down to ~5-10, which is still a significant win even if it's not as clean as a single batched launch.\n\nBut there's another wrinkle: `num_cosets` varies per AIR based on its maximum constraint degree, which means I can't just batch by `num_x` alone—I'd need to consider both dimensions when grouping.\n\nActually, the batched approach sidesteps this problem entirely. Instead of trying to fit everything into a 2D grid, I can use a 1D grid where each block handles one (AIR, coset) pair and looks up its parameters from a descriptor array, similar to how the MLE batch kernels use `BlockCtx`. The Round 0 constraint eval kernel would then iterate through AIRs and cosets, with each block fetching its context and executing the same computation.\n\nThe tricky part is that different AIRs have different `num_x` values, so blocks will do heterogeneous amounts of work. That's fine — CUDA handles it naturally. Within each AIR, blocks still need to cooperate for the reduction step, which the `air_block_offsets` array tracks to know how many blocks belong to each AIR.\n\nFor interaction evaluation, the main added complexity is constructing the per-AIR DAG structure, though caching the DAG could help avoid redundant work.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the actual timing at APC 300: Round 0 takes about 294ms total, with GPU kernel execution around 100ms wall time, buffer allocations and data transfers eating up roughly 105ms combined, CPU-side DAG construction and post-processing another 70ms, and synchronization overhead around 19ms. With batching, I can reduce the GPU kernel time to roughly 90ms by launching everything at once, which should bring the overall time down significantly.\n\nFor full batching across both constraints and interactions, I'm looking at about 120ms total—that's a savings of roughly 174ms compared to the unbatched approach. If I only batch the constraints, the interaction evaluation still runs per-AIR at around 130ms, giving me a total of 190ms and only 104ms in savings. So the full batching strategy nets an extra 70ms improvement over the constraints-only approach.\n\nThe main question is whether the added complexity is justified. Full batching requires caching the interaction DAG at keygen time, which is a bigger change to the proving key format. Constraint-only batching avoids that keygen modification entirely. But I could potentially cache the interaction DAG without changing the proving key format itself—just as an additional cached field. I need to check whether `logup_round0_buffer_size` is still in the codebase after the prealloc-round0-buffers task was reverted.\n\nLooking at the recent commits, that optimization was indeed reverted, so the field was removed. But I can add something similar back if needed. For a focused single task, I think batching Round 0 zerocheck constraint evaluation with descriptor arrays makes the most sense—it's the simpler half of the full optimization and doesn't require any keygen changes.\n\nBut wait, there's another angle worth considering: instead of batching kernels, what if I just cache the interaction DAG structure at keygen time and pre-allocate the encoded rules and weight template on the device? Then at prove time, I'd only need to upload the dynamic weights, which is much smaller. This would eliminate the CPU DAG construction overhead and most of the host-to-device transfer cost.\n\nWith the existing multi-stream parallelism, this could deliver meaningful improvements without the complexity of implementing a new batched kernel. I'm estimating around 40-60ms savings, mainly from cutting out the CPU DAG construction, and it's relatively straightforward to implement.\n\nThough looking at it more carefully, this is less impactful than kernel batching since the real bottleneck is buffer allocation and kernel launches, not just DAG construction. So I'm leaning toward the batching approach with descriptor arrays for Round 0 constraint evaluation, and ideally extending it to interaction evaluation too.\n\nActually, let me reconsider from a different angle: what if I increase the per-batch AIR count to trigger multi-threading more effectively? The current batching is memory-limited and creates small batches, but if I could process all AIRs in a single call, I might unlock better parallelism. The multi-threading decision seems to hinge on the total work count, so I need to look at how that threshold is set.\n\nLooking at the code, the threading logic checks if the total AIR count across the segment exceeds 100, and at APC 300 with ~310 AIRs per segment, it does spawn 8 threads. But each thread processes its chunk sequentially, handling one AIR at a time with full per-AIR overhead. So the real win would come from batching within each thread or across all threads with a single batched kernel launch.\n\nI'm committing to the descriptor-array batched Round 0 approach, starting with constraint evaluation.\n\nLet me check how the MLE batch kernel is structured to follow that same pattern.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2186,"cache_read_input_tokens":104745,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2186},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"599f050d-37ae-436a-962c-719a743f4f26"}
