{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KGAgwueaiXRHCXEu5zfjAu","type":"tool_result","content":"44\t) -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, Round0EvalError> {\n45\t    let constraints_dag = &pk.vk.symbolic_constraints;\n46\t    if constraints_dag.constraints.constraint_idx.is_empty() || num_cosets == 0 {\n47\t        // No plain AIR constraints, return empty buffer\n48\t        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n49\t    }\n50\t\n51\t    let rules = &pk.other_data.zerocheck_round0;\n52\t\n53\t    let buffer_size: u32 = rules.inner.buffer_size;\n54\t    let intermed_capacity = unsafe {\n55\t        _zerocheck_r0_intermediates_buffer_size(\n56\t            buffer_size,\n57\t            skip_domain,\n58\t            num_x,\n59\t            num_cosets,\n60\t            max_temp_bytes,\n61\t        )\n62\t    };\n63\t    let mut intermediates = if intermed_capacity > 0 {\n64\t        debug!(\"zerocheck:intermediates_capacity={intermed_capacity}\");\n65\t        DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(intermed_capacity)\n66\t    } else {\n67\t        DeviceBuffer::<F>::new()\n68\t    };\n69\t\n70\t    let temp_sums_buffer_capacity = unsafe {\n71\t        _zerocheck_r0_temp_sums_buffer_size(\n72\t            buffer_size,\n73\t            skip_domain,\n74\t            num_x,\n75\t            num_cosets,\n76\t            max_temp_bytes,\n77\t        )\n78\t    };\n79\t    debug!(\"zerocheck:temp_sums_buffer_capacity={temp_sums_buffer_capacity}\");\n80\t    let mut temp_sums_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(temp_sums_buffer_capacity);\n81\t    let used_temp_bytes =\n82\t        intermed_capacity * size_of::<F>() + temp_sums_buffer_capacity * size_of::<EF>();\n83\t    if used_temp_bytes > max_temp_bytes {\n84\t        // We do not error if the required bytes is greater than the requested max, but this may\n85\t        // lead to unexpected peak memory usage.\n86\t        warn!(\"zerocheck used_temp_bytes ({used_temp_bytes}) > max_temp_bytes ({max_temp_bytes})\");\n87\t    }\n88\t\n89\t    let preprocessed_ptr = pk\n90\t        .preprocessed_data\n91\t        .as_ref()\n92\t        .map(|cd| cd.trace.buffer().as_ptr())\n93\t        .unwrap_or(std::ptr::null());\n94\t\n95\t    let mut sp_evals =\n96\t        DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_cosets as usize * skip_domain as usize);\n97\t    // SAFETY:\n98\t    // - No bounds checks are done in this kernel. It fully assumes that the Rules are trusted and\n99\t    //   all nodes are valid.\n100\t    unsafe {\n101\t        zerocheck_ntt_eval_constraints(\n102\t            &mut temp_sums_buffer,\n103\t            &mut sp_evals,\n104\t            selectors_cube,\n105\t            preprocessed_ptr,\n106\t            main_parts,\n107\t            eq_cube,\n108\t            lambda_pows,\n109\t            public_values,\n110\t            &rules.inner.d_rules,\n111\t            &rules.inner.d_used_nodes,\n112\t            buffer_size,\n113\t            &mut intermediates,\n114\t            skip_domain,\n115\t            num_x,\n116\t            height,\n117\t            num_cosets,\n118\t            g_shift,\n119\t            max_temp_bytes,\n120\t        )?;\n121\t    }\n122\t\n123\t    Ok(sp_evals)\n124\t}\n125\t\n126\t/// Evaluate interaction constraints (excluding plain AIR constraints) for a single AIR, given\n127\t/// prepared trace input.\n128\t///\n129\t/// `constraints` includes interaction expressions for the AIR.\n130\t/// See [`crate::logup_zerocheck`] module docs for async-free/peak memory behavior.\n131\t#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n132\tpub fn evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n133\t    pk: &DeviceStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"4d7318d7-739e-43b1-aa21-bfe072016e4f","timestamp":"2026-04-13T10:16:08.056Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/round0.rs","content":") -> Result<DeviceBuffer<EF>, Round0EvalError> {\n    let constraints_dag = &pk.vk.symbolic_constraints;\n    if constraints_dag.constraints.constraint_idx.is_empty() || num_cosets == 0 {\n        // No plain AIR constraints, return empty buffer\n        return Ok(DeviceBuffer::new());\n    }\n\n    let rules = &pk.other_data.zerocheck_round0;\n\n    let buffer_size: u32 = rules.inner.buffer_size;\n    let intermed_capacity = unsafe {\n        _zerocheck_r0_intermediates_buffer_size(\n            buffer_size,\n            skip_domain,\n            num_x,\n            num_cosets,\n            max_temp_bytes,\n        )\n    };\n    let mut intermediates = if intermed_capacity > 0 {\n        debug!(\"zerocheck:intermediates_capacity={intermed_capacity}\");\n        DeviceBuffer::<F>::with_capacity(intermed_capacity)\n    } else {\n        DeviceBuffer::<F>::new()\n    };\n\n    let temp_sums_buffer_capacity = unsafe {\n        _zerocheck_r0_temp_sums_buffer_size(\n            buffer_size,\n            skip_domain,\n            num_x,\n            num_cosets,\n            max_temp_bytes,\n        )\n    };\n    debug!(\"zerocheck:temp_sums_buffer_capacity={temp_sums_buffer_capacity}\");\n    let mut temp_sums_buffer = DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(temp_sums_buffer_capacity);\n    let used_temp_bytes =\n        intermed_capacity * size_of::<F>() + temp_sums_buffer_capacity * size_of::<EF>();\n    if used_temp_bytes > max_temp_bytes {\n        // We do not error if the required bytes is greater than the requested max, but this may\n        // lead to unexpected peak memory usage.\n        warn!(\"zerocheck used_temp_bytes ({used_temp_bytes}) > max_temp_bytes ({max_temp_bytes})\");\n    }\n\n    let preprocessed_ptr = pk\n        .preprocessed_data\n        .as_ref()\n        .map(|cd| cd.trace.buffer().as_ptr())\n        .unwrap_or(std::ptr::null());\n\n    let mut sp_evals =\n        DeviceBuffer::<EF>::with_capacity(num_cosets as usize * skip_domain as usize);\n    // SAFETY:\n    // - No bounds checks are done in this kernel. It fully assumes that the Rules are trusted and\n    //   all nodes are valid.\n    unsafe {\n        zerocheck_ntt_eval_constraints(\n            &mut temp_sums_buffer,\n            &mut sp_evals,\n            selectors_cube,\n            preprocessed_ptr,\n            main_parts,\n            eq_cube,\n            lambda_pows,\n            public_values,\n            &rules.inner.d_rules,\n            &rules.inner.d_used_nodes,\n            buffer_size,\n            &mut intermediates,\n            skip_domain,\n            num_x,\n            height,\n            num_cosets,\n            g_shift,\n            max_temp_bytes,\n        )?;\n    }\n\n    Ok(sp_evals)\n}\n\n/// Evaluate interaction constraints (excluding plain AIR constraints) for a single AIR, given\n/// prepared trace input.\n///\n/// `constraints` includes interaction expressions for the AIR.\n/// See [`crate::logup_zerocheck`] module docs for async-free/peak memory behavior.\n#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\npub fn evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu<HS: GpuHashScheme>(\n    pk: &DeviceStarkProvingKey<GenericGpuBackend<HS>>,","numLines":90,"startLine":44,"totalLines":276}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NxnEqsBzavePtss8NPXBQk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the full picture. The zerocheck constraint DAG is already pre-computed in `pk.other_data.zerocheck_round0`. The interaction DAG is computed at prove time in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu`. \n\nFor the batching plan, the zerocheck constraint eval is straightforward because all the rules are already on-device in the proving key. The interaction eval requires either caching the interaction DAG or building it ahead of time.\n\nLet me now generate the ideas and select the best one. I'm confident that batching Round 0 constraint evaluation using descriptor arrays is the best next step. Let me estimate the impact more precisely.\n\n\nLooking at the profiling data from Round 0 at APC 300, the constraint evaluation kernels dominate the runtime—the parallel coset variant alone takes 238ms across 539 instances, with the other kernel variants contributing another 49ms combined. That's roughly 287ms total for constraint evaluation, which is a significant portion of the round's time and a prime target for batching optimization.\n\nThe interaction evaluation phase is similarly expensive at 277ms across 766 instances, with the parallel coset variant again being the bottleneck at 216ms for 396 instances. Combined, these two phases consume about 564ms of GPU time across roughly 1521 kernel invocations in Round 0 alone.\n\nLooking at the wall time of 294ms, I'm seeing an effective parallelism of about 1.92x, which suggests significant serialization overhead. If I could batch all the constraint evaluation kernels into a single launch, the GPU time would remain around 287ms but all blocks would execute concurrently across the 128 SMs without inter-kernel gaps or launch overhead, potentially achieving much better SM utilization. For the constraint evaluation specifically, I'm trying to figure out how much speedup is possible if all 539 coset-parallel instances could run simultaneously rather than sequentially—this depends on how many blocks each kernel uses, and with an average of 252μs per instance, a typical kernel might occupy 5-20 blocks, meaning each of my 8 threads would handle about 67 instances serially with launch and completion waits in between.\n\nActually, I'm realizing CUDA streams are asynchronous—kernel launches don't block the CPU, they just queue up. But the real bottleneck is that buffer allocation between kernel launches goes through the MemoryManager mutex with `cudaMallocAsync`, which is stream-ordered and serializes within a stream. So the actual constraints are the per-AIR CPU work, the mutex acquisitions for buffer allocation, and kernel launch overhead. Batching would eliminate all that per-AIR GPU overhead and replace it with a single batch setup.\n\nLooking at the Round 0 timing at APC 300, I'm seeing about 294ms total. If I batch just the constraint evaluation, I'd cut roughly half the per-AIR overhead, but the interaction evaluation would still have its own per-AIR costs. Breaking down the numbers: constraint eval is around 287ms, interaction eval is 277ms, then there's about 100ms of per-AIR CPU work, 80ms of per-AIR GPU overhead from allocation and transfers, 10ms for multi-threading coordination, totaling 294ms with 8-thread multi-streaming.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering what the GPU time actually represents. The 287ms reported by nsys is the wall-clock elapsed time on the GPU, not theoretical compute time—it's the sum of all 755 kernel instances running with overlap across 8 threads. If I batched all 755 instances into a single kernel launch, the GPU scheduler would assign all blocks at once to the SMs, and the total GPU time would be the total work divided by the number of available SMs.\n\nLooking at what the batched MLE kernels achieved for comparison: they went from 115ms down to 55ms (a 2.09x speedup) by reducing ~5K kernel instances to just 2 per round. For constraint eval with ~755 instances, if I could achieve a similar 2x improvement, that would save roughly 74ms from the current ~147ms contribution to Round 0's wall time. But the constraint and interaction evals actually interleave within each AIR, so the constraint eval doesn't have its own isolated time window to optimize.\n\nLet me approach this from first principles instead. Round 0 takes 294ms total with 8 threads processing ~39 AIRs each. If each AIR costs about 1600μs (600μs constraint eval + 500μs interaction eval + 500μs overhead), that's 62ms per thread. With perfect parallelism across 8 threads, I'd expect 62ms wall time, but I'm seeing 294ms—which suggests either my per-AIR estimate is significantly off or the threads are much more serialized than I thought. Dividing 294ms by 8 threads gives 37ms if they were perfectly parallel, but clearly there's substantial serialization happening.\n\nLooking at the actual GPU metrics, constraint evaluation averages 0.93ms per AIR and interaction evaluation averages 0.89ms per AIR, totaling about 1.8ms of GPU work per AIR. Yet each thread is spending roughly 7.5ms per AIR (294ms ÷ 39 AIRs), which means there's a significant gap between GPU time and actual thread time—likely from synchronization overhead, memory transfers, or CPU-side processing that's not overlapping well across threads.\n\nThe kernels themselves should pipeline nicely since each AIR's constraint evaluation is independent, allowing the CPU to set up the next AIR while the GPU processes the current one. But if buffer allocations through `cudaMallocAsync` are serializing across threads, that could be bottlenecking the pipeline and preventing the expected overlap.\n\nWith 8 threads all contending for the global mutex in MemoryManager, the CPU work gets serialized even though the GPU kernels could run concurrently. This matches what was discovered in the prealloc-gkr-input-buffers task—mutex contention was the main bottleneck, and pre-allocation eliminated over 1200 mutex acquisitions per segment. The Round 0 prealloc attempt failed because the buffer sizes were too large, but a batching approach could work differently by allocating smaller buffers dynamically rather than pre-sizing them to the maximum.\n\nInstead of pre-allocating max-sized buffers, I could allocate one intermediate buffer per AIR during CPU descriptor building and upload all contexts together. With batched kernels, all AIRs run simultaneously, so I'd need intermediate buffers for every AIR at once—at APC 300 with 310 AIRs, this could get very large. But the MLE batch kernels already handle this by allocating per-AIR intermediates and keeping them alive, with total memory bounded by batch size. For Round 0, I could sub-batch AIRs by total intermediate buffer size and launch one batched kernel per sub-batch, reducing the total kernel launches from many down to maybe 5-10 across all sub-batches.\n\nNow I'm checking how large these per-AIR intermediate buffers actually are. The capacity calculation depends on several factors like DAG node count, domain parameters, and temporary memory limits. For small AIRs at APC 300, these should be relatively small, with temporary memory budgeted around 80MB per thread based on the memory limit.\n\nIn a batched approach though, we'd work with a global memory budget instead of per-thread limits, so each small AIR's intermediate buffer would be even smaller. Looking at the prealloc report, the maximum buffer sizes across all AIRs are around 984MB per thread, but most small AIRs would use significantly less than that. If the average small AIR needs roughly 1MB of intermediates, then batching 310 AIRs would require only about 310MB total.\n\nThis seems workable within the 24GB GPU memory. So I'm settling on the most impactful optimization: batching the Round 0 zerocheck constraint evaluation using descriptor arrays. This targets the constraint eval specifically (not interaction eval), leverages the proven descriptor-array pattern from MLE batch kernels, eliminates per-AIR buffer allocations and kernel launches, groups AIRs by their `num_x` parameter, and falls back to sequential evaluation for large AIRs that already max out the GPU.\n\nI could also cache the interaction DAG structure at keygen and batch the interaction eval as a second step, but given the complexity, I'm focusing on constraint eval batching first. Looking at the numbers though—Round 0 constraint eval takes ~287ms at APC 300, and batching might save 80-100ms, bringing it down to 194-214ms. That's only an 80ms improvement overall, which makes me wonder if constraint eval batching alone is enough. The real bottleneck might be that interaction eval still has per-AIR overhead that serializes the threads, and since constraint and interaction evals run sequentially within each AIR, optimizing just one doesn't fully solve the problem.\n\nWith 39 AIRs and 6.6ms per AIR spent on interaction eval plus overhead, I'm looking at around 257ms total thread time even with instant constraint eval—barely better than the current 294ms. This tells me I actually need to batch both constraint and interaction evals together to see meaningful gains, which means either caching the interaction DAG at keygen or finding another way to eliminate that per-AIR overhead. The tradeoff is that caching the DAG at keygen is high complexity, requiring changes to keygen and two new batched CUDA kernels.\n\nAlternatively, I could pre-compute all interaction DAGs and rules on the main thread before spawning the parallel loop, then upload everything at once so each thread just launches kernels with pre-loaded data. This avoids keygen changes but still leaves kernel launches and buffer allocations as bottlenecks. Or I could step back entirely and look at optimizing a different component—Trace Commit is already scaling well at 2.16x from APC 0 to APC 300, so maybe WHIR is where the real gains are.\n\nActually, WHIR's also scaling well at 2.15x. The real bottlenecks are Round 0 and MLE Rounds, which are getting worse as APC increases, plus Stacked Reduction which is already heavily optimized and probably can't improve much further since the remaining 74ms is just the actual sumcheck compute time.\n\nFor MLE Rounds, the 169ms at APC 300 is mostly kernel execution time, not overhead. The rounds are inherently sequential because each one produces a challenge for the next, and within each round the kernel launches are already batched together, so there's limited parallelism left to extract. The slowdown at higher APC is likely because each round's kernels are doing more work.\n\nLet me move forward with my final decision.\n\nI'm going with optimizing Batch Round 0 to handle both constraint and interaction evaluation using descriptor arrays, with interaction rules cached at key generation. This targets the primary bottleneck at APC 300 where Round 0 dominates the timing. The expected improvement is substantial—bringing Round 0 down from 294ms to around 100-150ms—which would reduce total STARK execution time to roughly 1265-1315ms. While this alone won't quite reach the 2x improvement target, it's the most impactful single optimization available and gets us significantly closer to that goal.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the APC 0 numbers. At APC 0, Round 0 takes 177ms with 99 AIRs running sequentially, which works out to about 1.8ms of GPU time per AIR. The GPU stays busy while the CPU sets up the next AIR, so overhead is hidden. At APC 300, each AIR's GPU time drops to roughly 0.6ms on average with coset-parallel kernels, but the CPU overhead for DAG construction, allocation, and host-to-device copies takes longer than the kernel execution itself. This leaves the GPU idle between launches, making batching especially valuable at higher APC values where the GPU is underutilized.\n\nNow I'm wondering whether batching at APC 0 would actually hurt performance. With 99 AIRs split across 5 segments, that's about 20 AIRs per segment. If I batch those 20 AIRs into just 1-2 launches per segment, the GPU time should stay roughly the same or even improve slightly due to better streaming multiprocessor utilization. The per-segment overhead would drop from around 40ms to 5ms, but the intermediate buffers for these large AIRs could become quite large, which might offset some gains.\n\nThe real issue is memory: if I pre-allocate all intermediate buffers for a batched launch at APC 0, I could hit memory limits with those large AIRs. The fix is to sub-batch by memory budget—so instead of one big batch of 20 AIRs, I'd split them into 2-4 smaller sub-batches. This gives me 8-16 kernel launches per segment instead of 40, which is still a solid improvement without risking memory issues or regression.\n\nI'm satisfied this is the right direction. Now I need to write out the actual task and implementation plan.\n\nThe interaction DAG caching at keygen could be optional—the core optimization (batching constraint evaluation) works independently. But without batching the interaction eval too, the gains are limited since those threads still serialize. If I only batch constraint eval, I get maybe 20ms overhead plus 50ms GPU time, but the interaction eval still runs per-AIR and becomes the bottleneck. So I should probably tackle both together in the plan, or at least structure it so the interaction batching is a clear next step if the constraint batching alone doesn't deliver enough improvement. Batching both constraint and interaction evaluation gets me down to ~100ms total across all five phases—a savings of about 194ms compared to the current approach. That's a much more substantial improvement, though it requires restructuring the entire orchestration and writing new batched kernels for both operations.\n\nThe real bottleneck is whether I can cache the interaction DAG at key generation time. Let me look at how the DAG is currently built—I'm iterating through each interaction, adding expressions for the count and message fields to a symbolic DAG builder.\n\nAfter building the DAG, I sort and deduplicate the indices, then construct the final DAG object and compute the GPU rules from it. The key insight is that all of this—the DAG builder, the expression DAG, and the GPU rules—is completely deterministic and depends only on the symbolic constraints from the proving key, which never change.\n\nSo I can cache the encoded rules and the weight computation results directly.\n\nAt prove time, I'd iterate through the interactions and accumulate weights into numerator and denominator vectors using the cached rule indices, which is a straightforward and efficient operation.\n\nThe cached data I need to store per AIR includes the rules buffer on device, the buffer size, and two index mappings—one for count expressions and one for message fields—which means adding a new field to `AirDataGpu` during keygen.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the kernel design more carefully, specifically how to structure the batched round 0 zerocheck constraint evaluation kernel.\n\nThe current approach uses a 2D grid with one dimension for cosets and another for x-blocks, but for batching I'd switch to a 1D grid where each block looks up its AIR and position from a context structure. The challenge is that different AIRs might need different block sizes based on their `x_per_block` calculation, but all blocks in a single kernel launch must have the same thread count—some threads would just be idle for AIRs that need fewer.\n\nThe key insight is that `skip_domain` is fixed globally, but `x_per_block` varies per AIR depending on buffer size and memory constraints. In the batched version, each block can independently determine how many `num_x` iterations it processes based on its own buffer size, so smaller AIRs handle more iterations per block while larger ones handle fewer, all within the same fixed block size.\n\nFor Round 0 batched constraint evaluation, I need to iterate through each AIR during CPU descriptor building to compute how many blocks are needed. For each AIR, I calculate `x_per_block` from its buffer size and memory limits, then determine the total block count by dividing `num_x` by `x_per_block` and accounting for the 2D coset structure. Each block gets its own context entry recording which AIR it belongs to and its local block index.\n\nThe GPU kernel then reads its assigned block context and determines which coset and x-range it's responsible for by dividing and modding the local block index. It executes the same evaluation loop as before but writes results to a temporary buffer indexed by the block's offset. A separate reduction kernel then aggregates each AIR's temporary sums into the final output.\n\nThe main challenge here is that different blocks handle different numbers of x iterations depending on how `num_x` divides by `x_per_block`, but within a single block all threads iterate over the same range.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel configuration, the block size is computed as `skip_domain * x_per_block`, where `skip_domain` is typically 8 and `x_per_block` gets determined by the memory budget and buffer constraints.\n\nFor a batched version across all AIRs, I need to pick a fixed block size that works universally. The minimum viable is `skip_domain` (handling one x-value per block), which means more blocks overall but simpler scheduling. For small AIRs with 16 x-values across 3 cosets, that's around 48 blocks per AIR, scaling to roughly 15,000 blocks across all 310 AIRs.\n\nThe real constraint is occupancy—8 threads per block is too sparse for GPU efficiency. But in practice, the MLE kernels use larger thread counts (up to 256) by combining `skip_domain` with `x_per_block`. If I fix `x_per_block = 32`, that gives 256 threads per block (8 × 32), which is much better for occupancy while still keeping each block's work manageable.\n\nThe tricky part is the intermediate buffer layout. Since each block needs its own buffer region and the buffer size depends on `num_threads`, I need to figure out how to allocate and index these buffers consistently across the batch. Looking at the existing coset-parallel kernel, the intermediate buffer is indexed by thread ID within each block, so for the batched version I'd need to partition the buffer space by AIR and ensure each AIR's region accounts for its specific `num_threads` value. Looking at the buffer allocation and indexing more carefully, the intermediates buffer is allocated once per AIR and sized to accommodate all blocks, with each block writing to its own slice using its block index and thread ID as offsets. For batching multiple AIRs, I'd need a single consolidated intermediates buffer where each AIR gets its own allocated region.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the block size strategy—using `skip_domain` as the minimum gives 8 threads, but that's too small for good occupancy. I should aim for something like 256 threads per block by multiplying `skip_domain` by a fixed constant, though this means some blocks will have idle threads if an AIR has fewer x-values to process. Let me check what block sizes the existing kernels actually use to see if there's a pattern I should follow.\n\nLooking at the coset-parallel kernel, it adapts `x_per_block` based on buffer size and available threads—for small buffers it's constrained by the number of x-values, and for large buffers it's adjusted by memory constraints. With `skip_domain = 8` and a 256-thread limit, I could get `x_per_block` up to 32, so blocks typically range from 8 to 256 threads. For the batched version, fixing the block size at 256 would give consistent occupancy, and even though small AIRs would have idle threads, they'd just exit early. But there's a complication: the kernel iterates over all rules in the DAG for each block, and the number of rules varies significantly by AIR, so a block with 256 threads might be severely underutilized if there are only a few rules to process.\n\nThe core issue is that a fixed block size doesn't adapt well to the varying computational load across different AIRs. I could group AIRs by similar parameters—like buffer size range, number of cosets, and x-value count—and launch separate kernels for each group, which would reduce the number of groups while still allowing some specialization. Alternatively, I could use a minimal block size of 8 threads (matching `skip_domain`) with one x-value per block for maximum flexibility, though that sacrifices occupancy. At APC 300 with 310 AIRs averaging around 3 cosets and 16 x-values each, I need to figure out which trade-off makes sense.\n\nLooking at the numbers: that configuration would create roughly 14,880 blocks with 119K total threads across the GPU. With an RTX 4090's 128 SMs and a 16-block-per-SM scheduling limit, I'd get about 7.3 waves of execution. But 8 threads per block only gives 128 threads per SM—just 8.3% occupancy, which is far too low. I need a larger block size to improve occupancy while still handling the varying workloads efficiently.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the kernel structure itself. The current coset-parallel approach assigns one coset per block, with threads distributed across NTT indices and x-values. Each thread loops through its assigned x-values sequentially. The challenge is that simply increasing block size to 256 threads doesn't naturally fit this decomposition without restructuring how work gets distributed across threads and how the inner loops are organized. handles one NTT index and loops over multiple x-values. The block reduction accumulates across all threads (all NTT indices and all x-values).\n\nFor the batched version, I'd want each block to handle one (AIR, coset) pair. The block has `skip_domain * x_per_block_for_this_AIR` threads, but since we need a fixed block size, we'd use a fixed block size and compute the effective x_per_block from the block size.\n\nLet me use THREADS_PER_BLOCK = 256. Each block loads its AIR context, computes x_per_block = 256 / skip_domain, maps thread tid to (ntt_idx, x_offset), and loops over x-values with stride x_per_block. For AIRs with fewer x-values than the block can handle, some threads stay idle; for larger AIRs, threads iterate multiple times.\n\nWith 310 AIRs averaging 3 cosets each, that's roughly 930 blocks total. At 256 threads per block, I'm looking at around 238K threads across the grid, but the per-SM occupancy becomes tight—about 7 blocks per SM would exceed the 1536 thread limit, so realistically I'd fit around 6 blocks per SM.\n\nThe GPU would process these blocks in waves, with 930 blocks distributed across 128 SMs giving roughly 7-8 waves. I need to reconsider the actual kernel timing though, since processing 32x more x-values per block would significantly increase the per-block execution time. I'm realizing the 287ms measurement includes all 8 streams running in parallel, so the actual per-stream time is around 36ms—which is still much higher than my 4.9ms estimate, suggesting I'm underestimating the actual per-block overhead or missing something about how the GPU scheduling works.\n\nLooking at the 539 kernel instances averaging 441μs each, if they were all running concurrently on 128 SMs, I'd expect roughly 2.8ms of wall-clock time, but with 8 streams limiting concurrency to maybe 8 instances at a time, the actual execution is much more serialized than my calculation accounts for.\n\nWith a batched approach where all blocks run in a single kernel, the GPU scheduler could distribute the ~800 blocks across the 128 SMs more efficiently. The total time would depend on how the work distributes per block, which varies based on the number of iterations per AIR—and that's where I'm getting tangled up in the details.\n\nLet me simplify: if the serial sum is 238ms and I divide by the number of SMs, I get roughly 1.9ms as a theoretical minimum. But realistically, accounting for occupancy limits, load imbalance, and register pressure, the batched constraint eval kernel would probably run in 10-20ms, with the interaction eval kernel taking similar time. Adding overhead, Round 0 could drop from 294ms down to around 50-70ms—a 4-6x speedup. Though I'm realizing I haven't fully accounted for the D...\n\nNow factoring in the D2H copies and CPU post-processing: the batched kernel runs in 20-40ms, copying results back takes about 5ms, and CPU post-processing across all AIRs in parallel takes around 30ms, bringing the total to 55-75ms. That's still a massive win. The CPU post-processing seems to be a fixed ~30ms bottleneck regardless of parallelization approach, so that's the ceiling for improvement there.\n\nWith batching, I'm looking at roughly 30ms for GPU kernels, 10ms for D2H and overhead, and 30ms for CPU post-processing—totaling around 70ms for Round 0 versus the current 294ms. That's a 224ms improvement, which would bring the new STARK time down to 1185ms and get us to a 1.82x speedup ratio. We're getting close to that 2x goal.\n\nBut I should be more realistic about this. Adding buffers for imperfect load balancing, memory-based sub-batching, descriptor overhead, and conservative GPU estimates brings the total closer to 110ms, which still represents solid savings over the current approach.\n\nEven with these conservative margins, we're looking at around 1225ms for the STARK time and a 1.76x ratio—still a meaningful improvement. At APC 0, Round 0 should stay roughly the same or get slightly better since we're reducing kernel launches while the GPU work stays constant, so there's no regression risk there.\n\nNow I need to verify that batching the Round 0 interaction eval kernel is actually feasible. Examining the kernel signature...\n\nAnalyzing the per-AIR parameters...\n\nThe key insight is that most data pointers can stay on device, but the challenge-dependent weights need uploading for each AIR, while the interaction rules and buffer metadata can be cached from keygen. I'd structure this with a `Round0InteractionCtx` that bundles all per-AIR state together, letting us batch process multiple AIRs efficiently by uploading only what changes between them.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1264,"cache_read_input_tokens":117340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1264},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"529ba633-c61f-4b47-825f-0d8709f52e07"}
