{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TzJadoYDvnpnFfWvCZ7wP5","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"0fe6aa71-fbdc-4ddd-a10f-8d93a37e56cb","timestamp":"2026-04-13T10:21:14.800Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-13-1600-batch-round0-descriptor-arrays\n\nReplace per-AIR Round 0 kernel launches with batched descriptor-array kernels for both zerocheck constraint evaluation and logup interaction evaluation. Currently, each of the ~310 AIRs per segment at APC 300 gets 2+ individual kernel launches, buffer allocations, and H2D copies, resulting in ~1500 kernel launches per segment — nsys profiling shows 76% of Round 0 wall time (294ms) is overhead, not GPU compute. By caching the interaction DAG structure at keygen and batching all per-AIR contexts into a single H2D upload + single kernel launch (using the proven descriptor-array pattern from MLE batch kernels), we expect to reduce Round 0 from 294ms to ~100ms at APC 300, saving ~190ms from STARK excl trace.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload","+# 2026-04-13-1600-batch-round0-descriptor-arrays"," ","-Replace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched device upload per round. Currently, each MLE round iterates over ~310 traces (at APC 300) and calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` individually, issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` for a tiny buffer (~16-48 bytes) per trace. Across all rounds and segments, this totals ~3000-4000 unnecessary allocation+copy+free triplets, each also acquiring the global `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex. Consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading once eliminates this overhead, saving an estimated 20-40ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.","+Replace per-AIR Round 0 kernel launches with batched descriptor-array kernels for both zerocheck constraint evaluation and logup interaction evaluation. Currently, each of the ~310 AIRs per segment at APC 300 gets 2+ individual kernel launches, buffer allocations, and H2D copies, resulting in ~1500 kernel launches per segment — nsys profiling shows 76% of Round 0 wall time (294ms) is overhead, not GPU compute. By caching the interaction DAG structure at keygen and batching all per-AIR contexts into a single H2D upload + single kernel launch (using the proven descriptor-array pattern from MLE batch kernels), we expect to reduce Round 0 from 294ms to ~100ms at APC 300, saving ~190ms from STARK excl trace."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload\n\nReplace per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched device upload per round. Currently, each MLE round iterates over ~310 traces (at APC 300) and calls `main_ptrs.to_device()` individually, issuing a separate `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` for a tiny buffer (~16-48 bytes) per trace. Across all rounds and segments, this totals ~3000-4000 unnecessary allocation+copy+free triplets, each also acquiring the global `MEMORY_MANAGER` mutex. Consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading once eliminates this overhead, saving an estimated 20-40ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n"}}
