scriptor structs on the CPU, computing all `numer_weights`/`denom_weights` arrays (still ~290 weight computations per segment), and the descriptor upload.\n- The CPU post-processing (transpose, iDFT, polynomial construction) still runs per-AIR, now in Phase D. The prior optimization attempt (task `2026-04-12-2245`) showed this CPU work is not the bottleneck, so making it parallel via rayon should not hurt.\n\nThe 100ms target seems optimistic. A more realistic estimate would be 140-180ms, given that the single-kernel launch still has to do the same GPU work plus the new descriptor overhead.\n\n### 9. File path and line number accuracy\n\n- `mod.rs` line 899: The plan says \"Round 0 evaluation within `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`.\" The actual code at line 899 is `// Phase 1: Build work items (main thread, pure CPU)`. This is correct.\n- `mod.rs` lines 939-943: The plan says \"Choose num_threads = 8 if >= 100 AIRs, else 1.\" Actual code at lines 939-943 confirms this.\n- `mod.rs` lines 944-966: Multi-thread via `std::thread::scope`. Confirmed at lines 944-966.\n- `round0.rs` lines 31-124: `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu` is at lines 31-124. Confirmed.\n- `round0.rs` lines 132-275: `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` is at lines 132-275. Confirmed.\n- `round0.rs` lines 154-212: The plan references the DAG construction block. The actual block is at lines 161-206. Close but not exact -- line 154 is `// We create a new interactions DAG`, the `let (rules, ...) = {...}` block starts at line 161.\n\nMost line references are accurate.\n\n---\n\n## Improvements I would consider\n\n### A. Use the existing per-AIR launch path but batch only the setup overhead\n\nInstead of writing entirely new batched CUDA kernels (which introduces the `buffer_stride`, `d`-heterogeneity, and identity-coset problems), keep the existing coset-parallel kernel launches but:\n1. Cache the interaction DAG at keygen (Change 1, which is sound).\n2. Pre-compute all weights for all AIRs in a single CPU pass before launching any kernel.\n3. Batch the H2D uploads (all `main_parts`, all weights) into a single copy.\n4. Use CUDA graphs or simply issue all kernel launches back-to-back on the same stream without sync.\n\nThis eliminates DAG construction and most H2D overhead without the CUDA kernel rewrite. It is lower risk and could capture 60-70% of the claimed benefit.\n\n### B. Group small AIRs by `(skip_domain, num_cosets)` for the batched kernel\n\nIf proceeding with the batched kernel approach, group AIRs with identical `(skip_domain, num_cosets)` and launch one batched kernel per group. This makes the block size, `d` for reduction, and `buffer_stride` calculation uniform within each batch, eliminating findings 2, 3, and 4. The number of distinct `(skip_domain, num_cosets)` pairs is likely small (<10 at APC 300), so this adds minimal launch overhead.\n\n### C. Use `final_reduce_block_sums` (non-batched) per group instead of adapting `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`\n\nIf sub-batching by `(skip_domain, num_cosets)`, each group has uniform `d = num_cosets * skip_domain`. The existing non-batched `final_reduce_block_sums` (launched with `gridDim.x = d`) works directly. Just need `total_blocks` per group to be correct. This avoids writing any new reduction kernel.\n\n---\n\n## Assessment\n\nThere are **three design-level blockers**:\n\n1. **Finding 1**: The `Round0ZcCtx` struct uses the wrong eval context type (`EvalCoreCtx` from MLE instead of `NttEvalContext`-compatible fields). The CUDA/Rust FFI boundary would be incorrect as written.\n\n2. **Finding 3**: The intermediate buffer `buffer_stride` computation per AIR in the batched kernel is unspecified. Without this, blocks from different AIRs would corrupt each other's intermediate buffers.\n\n3. **Finding 4**: The reduction kernel cannot handle per-AIR heterogeneous `d = num_cosets * skip_domain` with the existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums`. The plan must either specify a new kernel or a sub-batching strategy.\n\n**The plan is not prototype-ready.** An implementer would have to solve findings 1, 3, and 4 from scratch, which means the core CUDA design is unfinished.\n\n**The strongest part of the plan** is Change 1 (interaction DAG caching at keygen). This is correct, well-scoped, and delivers real value regardless of the batching approach. It should be implemented first as a standalone change.\n\nThe overall batching strategy is sound in concept -- the descriptor-array pattern from batch MLE is proven to work, and the bottleneck analysis (overhead from ~1500 kernel launches and ~2000 buffer allocations) is well-supported by the nsys data and the project's optimization history. The plan needs a second pass to resolve the three blockers, most likely by adopting sub-batching by `(skip_domain, num_cosets)` groups (Improvement B), which would make all three blockers disappear."}],"totalDurationMs":170650,"totalTokens":93622,"totalToolUseCount":29,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1120,"cache_read_input_tokens":88760,"output_tokens":3741,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1120},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3741,"cache_read_input_tokens":88760,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1120,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":19,"searchCount":10,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
