{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EnZPKTVWM1cN4sGs6dtnPT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review Guideline\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReview an implementation plan against the current codebase and existing measurements.\n6\t\n7\tThe job is not to paraphrase the plan. The job is to find:\n8\t\n9\t- correctness holes\n10\t- missing invariants\n11\t- interface mismatches with the current code\n12\t- places where estimated wins are not supported by measurements\n13\t- places where the plan is overcomplicated relative to expected proof-time benefit\n14\t- possible simplifications\n15\t\n16\t## Core standard\n17\t\n18\tA good review answers four questions:\n19\t\n20\t1. Is the proposal correct against the code that exists today?\n21\t2. Is it specific enough to implement a prototype without inventing missing design later?\n22\t3. Is it likely to improve the measured bottleneck it claims to target?\n23\t4. Is the proposed complexity justified by expected wall-clock benefit?\n24\t\n25\t## Review method\n26\t\n27\t### 1. Read the plan for concrete claims\n28\t\n29\tExtract:\n30\t\n31\t- the claimed bottleneck\n32\t- the proposed mechanism\n33\t- what is said to be AIR-level vs trace-level\n34\t- what is said to be reused, cached, batched, or hoisted\n35\t- expected savings and what units they use\n36\t\n37\tDo not accept vague claims like “should help” or “future cleanup will handle this.”\n38\t\n39\t### 2. Check the current code path end to end\n40\t\n41\tFind the actual call chain in the current implementation:\n42\t\n43\t- caller\n44\t- helper\n45\t- FFI boundary\n46\t- CUDA/kernel side if relevant\n47\t- postprocess / D2H / synchronization points\n48\t\n49\tVerify that the plan matches the real architecture, not an assumed one.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Check measurements before trusting priorities\n52\t\n53\tUse existing profiling or instrumentation first.\n54\t\n55\tFor each claimed win, ask:\n56\t\n57\t- is this work actually inside the measured bottleneck?\n58\t- is the cited span wall-clock or only a subspan?\n59\t- is the estimate per segment, per round, or per proof?\n60\t\n61\tReject priority arguments that contradict the data.\n62\t\n63\t### 4. Separate design blockers from cleanup\n64\t\n65\tA design blocker is something that makes the proposal:\n66\t\n67\t- incorrect\n68\t- unimplementable as written\n69\t- likely to fail at runtime\n70\t- likely to miss the intended performance target\n71\t\n72\tCleanup issues are:\n73\t\n74\t- wording imprecision\n75\t- stale checklist items\n76\t- missing explicit `Default` impl mention\n77\t- test/checklist polish\n78\t\n79\tBe strict about the difference.\n80\t\n81\t## What to verify every time\n82\t\n83\t### Correctness / architecture\n84\t\n85\t- Does the proposal preserve current invariants?\n86\t- Does it account for all inputs needed to reproduce current behavior?\n87\t- Does it match current buffer layout, indexing, launch geometry, and reduction shape?\n88\t- Does it accidentally rely on information only available on the CUDA side or only on the Rust side?\n89\t- Does it handle fallback paths, not just the new fast path?\n90\t\n91\t### Memory / buffers\n92\t\n93\t- Is capacity confused with logical length?\n94\t- Are reused buffers read back to host with the correct live length?\n95\t- Does the plan assume async frees happen immediately?\n96\t- Is the memory budget a real bound or just a heuristic?\n97\t- If sub-batching is proposed, is the scope of reuse actually large enough to matter?\n98\t\n99\t### Performance realism\n100\t\n101\t- Is the plan attacking the span that is actually large in Nsight/NVTX?\n102\t- Are claimed savings plausible relative to the cited measured span?\n103\t- Is the proposal removing work, or only moving it around?\n104\t- Is the proposal saving GPU time, CPU time, runtime API overhead, or sync time? Be explicit.\n105\t\n106\t### Implementation readiness\n107\t\n108\t- Are required new structs/helpers/FFI changes fully specified?\n109\t- Are raw pointers, lifetimes, or ownership changes spelled out?\n110\t- Does the checklist include all touched paths?\n111\t- Are there hidden “to be solved later” gaps in core behavior?\n112\t\n113\t## Preferred review output structure\n114\t\n115\tUse:\n116\t\n117\t1. `Findings`\n118\t2. `Improvements I would consider`\n119\t3. `Assessment`\n120\t\n121\tUnder `Findings`:\n122\t\n123\t- order by severity\n124\t- use precise statements\n125\t- include file references\n126\t- say exactly what is wrong and what must change\n127\t\n128\tUnder `Improvements I would consider`:\n129\t\n130\t- only include real alternatives that could better improve proof time\n131\t- distinguish these from blockers\n132\t\n133\tUnder `Assessment`:\n134\t\n135\t- state whether there is a remaining design-level blocker\n136\t- state whether the plan is prototype-ready\n137\t- say what the strongest part of the plan is\n138\t\n139\t## Writing rules\n140\t\n141\t- Be concrete, not rhetorical.\n142\t- Prefer “As written, X is wrong because Y in current code does Z.”\n143\t- Do not say “looks good overall” before findings.\n144\t- Do not invent performance numbers.\n145\t- Do not call something “overengineering” unless you explain what simpler path exists.\n146\t- Do not let “future work” hide a present correctness or implementation gap.\n147\t- If the proposal is good, say so plainly after checking it.\n148\t\n149\t## Useful review heuristics\n150\t\n151\t- Cached data must include every static input needed to rebuild current behavior.\n152\t- Reuse scoped inside a function call does not help if the costly repetition happens across calls.\n153\t- Exact-size host copies matter when buffer APIs do not track logical length separately.\n154\t- A plan that ignores fallback paths is usually incomplete.\n155\t- A measured `~100us` span is not a serious optimization target inside a `~10ms+` phase.\n156\t- The cleanest prototype path is usually the one that matches an existing pattern already used elsewhere in the codebase.\n157\t\n158\t## Minimum bar before approving a prototype plan\n159\t\n160\tApprove only if all of these are true:\n161\t\n162\t- no remaining design-level correctness blocker\n163\t- no missing data needed to reproduce current behavior\n164\t- no hidden interface gap between Rust and CUDA\n165\t- the intended win targets a measured bottleneck\n166\t- the checklist is sufficient for someone else to implement the prototype without guessing\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DJZrRxfovuAPecnesbZeka","session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"0eeb4424-aa21-44cb-a306-362034b3c8de","timestamp":"2026-04-13T13:06:28.324Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019SHPhZfQjyXHo3c69St55o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch Round 0 Descriptor Arrays\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-AIR Round 0 kernel launches (zerocheck constraint eval + logup interaction eval) with batched descriptor-array kernel launches, eliminating ~1500 kernel launches per segment at APC 300. This directly targets the worst-scaling component: Round 0 takes 294ms at APC 300 vs 177ms at APC 0 (gets SLOWER with more APCs), with nsys profiling showing 76% of wall time is per-AIR overhead (buffer allocation, kernel launch, H2D/D2H copies, CPU DAG construction), not GPU compute.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry Point\n10\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs` line 899: Round 0 evaluation within `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`.\n11\t\n12\t### Main Loop (lines 899-966)\n13\t1. **Phase 1 (lines 899-926)**: Build `Vec<Round0AirWorkItem>` — one per trace. Pure CPU.\n14\t2. **Line 929**: Sort work items by descending height.\n15\t3. **Lines 939-943**: Choose `num_threads = 8` if ≥ 100 AIRs, else 1.\n16\t4. **Lines 944-966**: Multi-thread via `std::thread::scope`. Each thread processes its chunk of AIRs sequentially via `process_air_round0()`.\n17\t\n18\t### Per-AIR Processing: `process_air_round0()` (lines 140-272)\n19\tFor each AIR, sequentially:\n20\t1. `SymbolicConstraints::from()` — CPU: build constraint representation from proving key (line 144)\n21\t2. `main_parts.to_device()` — H2D: upload trace pointers (line 162)\n22\t3. `evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu()` — GPU: 1 eval kernel + 1 reduction kernel + 3 buffer allocations (intermediates, temp_sums, sp_evals) (lines 168-181, defined in round0.rs lines 31-124)\n23\t4. `sum_buffer.to_host_on_current_stream()` — D2H (line 184)\n24\t5. CPU: transpose + iDFT + polynomial construction (lines 185-208)\n25\t6. `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu()` — CPU: build synthetic interaction DAG + compute weights (round0.rs lines 161-206). GPU: 1 eval kernel + 1 reduction kernel + 5+ buffer allocations (round0.rs lines 209-272)\n26\t7. `sum.to_host_on_current_stream()` — D2H (line 232)\n27\t8. CPU: unzip, transpose, iDFT, polynomial construction (lines 233-261)\n28\t\n29\t### Bottleneck Analysis (APC 300, 310 AIRs/segment)\n30\tPer segment, the current code executes:\n31\t- ~620 kernel launches (310 AIRs × 2 evals, each with eval + reduction)\n32\t- ~2000+ DeviceBuffer allocations (via global MemoryManager mutex)\n33\t- ~620 H2D copies (main_parts, rules, weights per AIR)\n34\t- ~620 D2H copies (results per AIR)\n35\t- ~310 CPU DAG constructions for interaction eval (SymbolicDagBuilder + SymbolicRulesGpu::new)\n36\t\n37\tNsys data: Total Round 0 GPU kernel time = 564ms across all streams. With 8 threads, ideal wall time would be ~30ms. Actual = 294ms. Overhead = 264ms (76%).\n38\t\n39\t### CUDA Kernels\n40\t- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`: `zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_coset_parallel_kernel` — 539 instances, 238ms total GPU time. Block size determined by `MAX_THREADS = 128` (line 472) and `eval_config.cuh` `kernel_launch_params()`.\n41\t- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`: `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_coset_parallel_kernel` — 396+339=735 instances, 249ms total GPU time. Block size determined by `MAX_THREADS = 128` (line 466).\n42\t\n43\t### Key Data Structures in Current Kernels\n44\tThe coset-parallel kernels use `NttEvalContext<1>` (from `dag_entry.cuh`), which operates on base-field `Fp` data with NTT-domain-specific fields: `omega_shifts`, `ntt_buffer`, `ntt_idx`, `buffer_stride`. This is fundamentally different from `EvalCoreCtx` (from `eval_ctx.cuh`), which operates on extension-field `FpExt` data in the MLE batch kernels.\n45\t\n46\tThe intermediates buffer layout is `[buffer_size][total_threads_for_air]` where `total_threads_for_air = num_cosets * gridDim.x * blockDim.x`. Each thread accesses `d_intermediates + global_tidx` with stride `buffer_stride = total_threads_for_air` (see `zerocheck_round0.cu` lines 399-403).\n47\t\n48\t### Existing Pattern to Follow\n49\t`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs` lines 113-218: `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder` — collects per-AIR `BlockCtx` + `ZerocheckCtx` on CPU, uploads once, launches one batched kernel for all AIRs. Uses `air_block_offsets` for the reduction phase.\n50\t\n51\t## Changes\n52\t\n53\t### Change 1: Cache interaction DAG structure at keygen\n54\t\n55\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/pkey.rs`\n56\t\n57\tAdd a new struct to `AirDataGpu`:\n58\t```rust\n59\tpub struct Round0InteractionRules {\n60\t    /// Encoded interaction DAG rules, pre-computed and uploaded at keygen.\n61\t    pub d_rules: DeviceBuffer<u128>,\n62\t    pub buffer_size: u32,\n63\t    /// For each interaction: the rule_idx of its `count` expression.\n64\t    pub count_rule_idxs: Vec<usize>,\n65\t    /// For each interaction: the rule_idxs of its message field expressions.\n66\t    /// message_rule_idxs[interaction_idx][field_idx] = rule_idx\n67\t    pub message_rule_idxs: Vec<Vec<usize>>,\n68\t    /// Number of rules (= length of weight vectors).\n69\t    pub rules_len: usize,\n70\t}\n71\t```\n72\t\n73\tAdd field `pub interaction_round0: Option<Round0InteractionRules>` to `AirDataGpu`.\n74\t\n75\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/pkey.rs` (in the keygen function that populates `AirDataGpu`)\n76\t\n77\tFor each AIR with interactions, run the same DAG construction currently in `evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu` (round0.rs lines 161-206):\n78\t1. Build `SymbolicDagBuilder` from `symbolic.interactions`\n79\t2. Create `SymbolicRulesGpu::new()`\n80\t3. Encode and upload rules to device\n81\t4. Extract `dag_idx_to_rule_idx` mapping for count and message expressions\n82\t5. Store as `Round0InteractionRules`\n83\t\n84\tThis moves ~310 DAG constructions per segment from prove-time to keygen-time (once per AIR, amortized).\n85\t\n86\t### Change 2: Add NTT-compatible descriptor structs\n87\t\n88\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`\n89\t\n90\tAdd per-AIR descriptor struct using base-field `Fp` types (NOT `EvalCoreCtx` which uses `FpExt`):\n91\t```cuda\n92\tstruct Round0ZcCtx {\n93\t    // NTT-domain base-field pointers (matching NttEvalContext<1> needs)\n94\t    const Fp *selectors_cube;        // [3][num_x]\n95\t    const Fp *preprocessed;          // preprocessed trace (nullable)\n96\t    const Fp *const *main_parts;     // array of base-field trace buffer pointers\n97\t    const FpExt *eq_cube;            // eq(x) evaluations [num_x]\n98\t    const FpExt *lambda_pows;        // lambda challenge powers\n99\t    const Fp *public_values;         // per-AIR public inputs\n100\t    const Rule *d_rules;             // constraint DAG rules (from pkey)\n101\t    const size_t *d_used_nodes;      // constraint node indices (from pkey)\n102\t    size_t rules_len;\n103\t    size_t used_nodes_len;\n104\t    size_t lambda_len;\n105\t    uint32_t buffer_size;            // DAG intermediate buffer size\n106\t    Fp *d_intermediates;             // offset into shared intermediates buffer\n107\t    uint32_t buffer_stride;          // total threads for this AIR = num_cosets * num_x_blocks * block_x\n108\t    uint32_t num_x;\n109\t    uint32_t height;\n110\t    Fp g_shift;                      // coset generator\n111\t};\n112\t```\n113\t\n114\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n115\t\n116\tAnalogous `Round0LogupCtx` with additional fields:\n117\t```cuda\n118\tstruct Round0LogupCtx {\n119\t    // Same base-field pointers as Round0ZcCtx\n120\t    const Fp *selectors_cube;\n121\t    const Fp *preprocessed;\n122\t    const Fp *const *main_parts;\n123\t    const FpExt *eq_cube;\n124\t    const Fp *public_values;\n125\t    const Rule *d_rules;             // interaction DAG rules (from keygen cache)\n126\t    size_t rules_len;\n127\t    uint32_t buffer_size;\n128\t    Fp *d_intermediates;\n129\t    uint32_t buffer_stride;\n130\t    const FpExt *numer_weights;      // per-rule numerator weights (uploaded per segment)\n131\t    const FpExt *denom_weights;      // per-rule denominator weights (uploaded per segment)\n132\t    FpExt denom_sum_init;            // scalar per AIR\n133\t    uint32_t num_x;\n134\t    uint32_t height;\n135\t    Fp g_shift;\n136\t};\n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tBoth files: reuse the existing `BlockCtx` struct from `batch_mle.cu` (or define a compatible `struct { uint32_t local_block_idx; uint32_t air_idx; }`).\n140\t\n141\t### Change 3: Sub-batch by (skip_domain, num_cosets) groups\n142\t\n143\t**Critical design decision**: Different AIRs have different `skip_domain` (= `1 << l_skip`, but `l_skip` is global so this is actually uniform) and different `num_cosets` (= `constraint_degree - 1` for zerocheck, `constraint_degree` for logup). Within a batch, all AIRs must share the same `num_cosets` to:\n144\t- Allow uniform block size (`skip_domain * x_per_block`)\n145\t- Allow uniform `d = num_cosets * skip_domain` for the reduction kernel\n146\t- Simplify intermediate buffer stride computation\n147\t\n148\t**Grouping strategy**:\n149\t1. Partition small AIRs by `num_cosets_zc` for constraint eval groups, and by `num_cosets_logup` for interaction eval groups.\n150\t2. At APC 300, `num_cosets` is typically 2-4 (constraint degree 3-5). Expected ~3 groups.\n151\t3. Each group gets one batched kernel launch + one reduction kernel launch.\n152\t4. Total: ~6 constraint launches + ~6 interaction launches = ~12 launches vs current ~1500.\n153\t\n154\t**Block size per group**: `block_x = min(skip_domain * floor(MAX_THREADS / skip_domain), skip_domain * max_x_for_group)` where `MAX_THREADS = 128` (from zerocheck_round0.cu line 472) and `max_x_for_group = max(num_x)` across AIRs in the group. For `skip_domain = 8`, `block_x = min(128, 8 * max_x) = 128` typically (since `max_x >= 16` for most groups). `x_per_block = block_x / skip_domain = 16`.\n155\t\n156\t### Change 4: Write batched Round 0 zerocheck constraint eval kernel\n157\t\n158\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/zerocheck_round0.cu`\n159\t\n160\tNew kernel: `batched_zerocheck_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`\n161\t- **Grid**: `(total_blocks, 1)` — 1D grid\n162\t- **Block**: `block_x` threads (uniform within group, computed per Change 3)\n163\t- **Template parameter**: `<bool GLOBAL>` — always `true` for the batched kernel (use device memory for intermediates; no shared memory variant needed)\n164\t- **Per-block logic**:\n165\t  1. Load `BlockCtx` from `d_block_ctxs[blockIdx.x]` → get `air_idx`, `local_block_idx`\n166\t  2. Load `Round0ZcCtx` from `d_zc_ctxs[air_idx]`\n167\t  3. From group-uniform `num_cosets` and per-AIR `num_x`: compute `num_x_blocks = ceil(num_x / x_per_block)`, then `coset_idx = local_block_idx / num_x_blocks`, `x_block_idx = local_block_idx % num_x_blocks`\n168\t  4. `x_per_block = blockDim.x / skip_domain` (known from block size)\n169\t  5. Thread maps: `ntt_idx = threadIdx.x % skip_domain`, `x_offset = threadIdx.x / skip_domain + x_block_idx * x_per_block`\n170\t  6. Compute NTT evaluation context: `inter_buffer = ctx.d_intermediates + (coset_idx * num_x_blocks * blockDim.x + x_block_idx * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x)`. Stride = `ctx.buffer_stride`.\n171\t  7. Compute coset-specific values: `g_coset = pow(g_shift, coset_idx + 1)` (matching zerocheck_round0.cu line 388 — no identity-coset special case for zerocheck, unlike logup). Precompute `is_first_mult`, `is_last_mult` from `g_coset` (lines 389-393 of zerocheck_round0.cu).\n172\t  8. Main loop: iterate `x_int` from `x_offset` to `num_x` stepping by `x_per_block`. Call `acc_constraints<1, false>(...)` (GLOBAL=true, no shmem) per iteration. Accumulate into `sum`.\n173\t  9. Block-level reduction across `skip_domain` threads, write to `tmp_sums_buffer[block_offset + coset_idx * skip_domain + ntt_idx]` where `block_offset` is the per-AIR offset.\n174\t\n175\tNew launcher: `_batched_zerocheck_r0_eval_constraints`\n176\t- Parameters: `d_block_ctxs`, `d_zc_ctxs`, `total_blocks`, `block_x`, `num_cosets`, `skip_domain`, `d_air_offsets`, `d_tmp_sums`, `d_output`, `num_airs`\n177\t- Launches batched eval kernel + `final_reduce_block_sums` per-AIR reduction with uniform `d = num_cosets * skip_domain`\n178\t\n179\t**Reduction kernel**: Use the existing `sumcheck::batched_final_reduce_block_sums` from `sumcheck.cuh` directly. Since all AIRs in a group share the same `num_cosets` and `skip_domain`, `d = num_cosets * skip_domain` is uniform. Grid: `(num_airs, d)`. The `air_block_offsets` array gives the block range per AIR.\n180\t\n181\t### Change 5: Write batched Round 0 logup interaction eval kernel\n182\t\n183\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/logup_round0.cu`\n184\t\n185\tNew kernel: `batched_logup_r0_coset_parallel_kernel`\n186\t- Same 1D grid structure as Change 4\n187\t- Per-block: load `Round0LogupCtx`, compute coset/x indices\n188\t- **Identity-coset handling**: coset_idx 0 is the identity coset. The kernel computes `bool is_identity_coset = (coset_idx == 0)` and passes it as the runtime `skip_ntt` flag to `acc_interactions<1, false, false>(...)`, matching the existing coset-parallel logup kernel logic (logup_round0.cu lines 361-442). For coset_idx 0: `g_coset = Fp::one()` and `skip_ntt = true`. For coset_idx > 0: `g_coset = g_shift^(...)` and `skip_ntt = false`.\n189\t- Block-level reduction writes `FracExt` (numer + denom) to `tmp_sums_buffer`\n190\t\n191\tNew launcher: `_batched_logup_r0_eval_interactions`\n192\t- Same structure as Change 4 launcher.\n193\t- **FracExt reduction**: `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` is typed for `FpExt`, not `FracExt`. Apply the same stacked reinterpret pattern used in the existing non-batched logup round0 launchers (logup_round0.cu lines 579, 651): `reinterpret_cast<FpExt*>(tmp_sums_buffer)` and `reinterpret_cast<FpExt*>(output)`, then set `d = 2 * num_cosets * skip_domain` (doubling `d` because `FracExt = {FpExt p, FpExt q}` is layout-compatible with `FpExt[2]`). The `air_block_offsets` remain unchanged (they count blocks, which are the same regardless of element type). Grid: `(num_airs, 2 * num_cosets * skip_domain)`.\n194\t\n195\t### Change 6: Rust orchestration — batched Round 0\n196\t\n197\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n198\t\n199\tReplace the multi-threaded per-AIR loop (lines 935-966) with a batched approach. New helper function `evaluate_round0_batched()`:\n200\t\n201\t**Phase A: Classify and group AIRs (single thread, CPU only)**\n202\tFor each work item:\n203\t1. Compute `num_x = 1 << n_lift`, `num_cosets_zc = local_constraint_deg - 1`, `num_cosets_logup = local_constraint_deg`\n204\t2. Check coset-parallel threshold: `num_x * skip_domain < 32768` (from `use_coset_parallel_mode()` in zerocheck_round0.cu line 477). If false → \"large AIR\", process individually via existing per-AIR `process_air_round0()` (Change 7).\n205\t3. Group small AIRs by `num_cosets_zc` for constraint eval groups.\n206\t4. Group small AIRs by `num_cosets_logup` for interaction eval groups.\n207\t5. Within each group, sort by num_x descending for load balance.\n208\t\n209\t**Phase B: Build descriptors per group (single thread, CPU only)**\n210\tFor each constraint eval group (all AIRs with same `num_cosets_zc`):\n211\t1. Compute `block_x = min(MAX_THREADS, skip_domain * max_num_x)` where `MAX_THREADS = 128`. Ensure `block_x` is a multiple of `skip_domain`.\n212\t2. `x_per_block = block_x / skip_domain`\n213\t3. For each AIR in group:\n214\t   a. `num_x_blocks = ceil(num_x / x_per_block)`\n215\t   b. `blocks_per_air = num_x_blocks * num_cosets_zc`\n216\t   c. Build `BlockCtx` entries (local_block_idx 0..blocks_per_air, air_idx)\n217\t   d. Compute intermediate buffer size: call `_zerocheck_r0_intermediates_buffer_size(buffer_size, skip_domain, num_x, num_cosets_zc, usize::MAX)`. **Critical**: pass `usize::MAX` as `max_temp_bytes` to prevent `eval_config.cuh` (lines 47-55) from reducing `grid.x` below `num_x_blocks`. Memory is controlled at the group level by the 2 GB cap (Phase C), not per-AIR via `max_temp_bytes`. This ensures the intermediates buffer is sized for the full `num_x_blocks`, matching the `buffer_stride` computation. Accumulate total.\n218\t   e. Compute `buffer_stride = num_cosets_zc * num_x_blocks * block_x` (must match the intermediates allocation from step d — both use the unreduced `num_x_blocks`)\n219\t   f. Build `Round0ZcCtx` with all pointers from work item + pkey, store placeholder for `d_intermediates` (backfilled in Phase C)\n220\t   g. Compute tmp_sums buffer contribution: `num_blocks_for_this_air * num_cosets_zc * skip_domain`\n221\t4. Record `air_offsets` (cumulative block counts for reduction)\n222\t\n223\tFor each interaction eval group (all AIRs with same `num_cosets_logup`):\n224\t1. Same block sizing as above with `num_cosets_logup`\n225\t2. For each AIR:\n226\t   a. Load cached rules from `pk.other_data.interaction_round0`\n227\t   b. Compute dynamic weights from `eq_3bs` and `beta_pows` using cached `count_rule_idxs` and `message_rule_idxs`:\n228\t      ```rust\n229\t      let mut numer_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; cached.rules_len];\n230\t      let mut denom_weights = vec![EF::ZERO; cached.rules_len];\n231\t      let mut denom_sum_init = EF::ZERO;\n232\t      for (i, interaction) in symbolic.interactions.iter().enumerate() {\n233\t          numer_weights[cached.count_rule_idxs[i]] += eq_3bs[i];\n234\t          denom_sum_init += eq_3bs[i] * beta_pows[interaction.message.len()]\n235\t              * F::from_u32(interaction.bus_index as u32 + 1);\n236\t          for (j, _) in interaction.message.iter().enumerate() {\n237\t              denom_weights[cached.message_rule_idxs[i][j]] += eq_3bs[i] * beta_pows[j];\n238\t          }\n239\t      }\n240\t      ```\n241\t   c. Append weights to flat host-side weight arrays\n242\t   d. Build `Round0LogupCtx` with pointers from work item + pkey + weight offsets\n243\t\n244\t**Phase C: Allocate, backfill, and upload per group**\n245\tFor each group:\n246\t1. **Memory budget check**: If total intermediate buffer > 2 GB, split group into sub-groups. The 2 GB threshold is chosen because: the prealloc-round0-buffers failure showed ~1 GB per thread (8 threads = 8 GB total) degraded all phases. Here, 2 GB is a single allocation, no per-thread multiplication. This is conservative relative to 24 GB GPU memory minus ~5-8 GB working set.\n247\t2. Allocate `intermediates: DeviceBuffer<F>` with total capacity for all AIRs in (sub-)group\n248\t3. Backfill `d_intermediates` pointers in context structs using running offset\n249\t4. Allocate `tmp_sums_buffer` and `output_buffer`\n250\t5. Upload: `d_block_ctxs`, `d_zc_ctxs` (or `d_logup_ctxs`), `d_air_offsets`, weight buffers (for logup groups)\n251\t\n252\t**Phase D: Launch kernels per group**\n253\t1. Launch `_batched_zerocheck_r0_eval_constraints` (or `_batched_logup_r0_eval_interactions`)\n254\t2. Launch reduction kernel: `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` with uniform `d`. For zerocheck groups: `d = num_cosets_zc * skip_domain` on `FpExt` buffers. For logup groups: `d = 2 * num_cosets_logup * skip_domain` on `reinterpret_cast<FpExt*>` of `FracExt` buffers (the stacked reinterpret pattern).\n255\t3. D2H copy output buffer\n256\t\n257\t**Phase E: CPU post-processing (parallel via rayon)**\n258\tFor each small AIR:\n259\t1. Extract constraint result slice from group's output buffer (offset = `air_idx * num_cosets_zc * skip_domain`)\n260\t2. Transpose + iDFT → zerocheck polynomial (same as current lines 185-208)\n261\t3. Extract interaction result slice similarly\n262\t4. Unzip Frac, transpose + iDFT → logup numer/denom polynomials (same as current lines 233-261)\n263\t\n264\t### Change 7: Fallback for large AIRs\n265\t\n266\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n267\t\n268\tAIRs failing the coset-parallel threshold (`num_x * skip_domain >= 32768`) use the existing per-AIR `process_air_round0()` sequentially on the main thread. These are few in number (typically < 20 at APC 300, ~20 at APC 0) and already saturate the GPU individually.\n269\t\n270\tProcess large AIRs BEFORE the batched groups, sequentially. This ensures they get full GPU bandwidth before the batched launches occupy it.\n271\t\n272\t### Change 8: Rust FFI bindings\n273\t\n274\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n275\t\n276\tAdd `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs matching the CUDA descriptors:\n277\t```rust\n278\t#[repr(C)]\n279\tpub struct Round0ZcCtx {\n280\t    pub selectors_cube: *const F,\n281\t    pub preprocessed: *const F,\n282\t    pub main_parts: *const *const F,\n283\t    pub eq_cube: *const EF,\n284\t    pub lambda_pows: *const EF,\n285\t    pub public_values: *const F,\n286\t    pub d_rules: *const u128,          // Rule encoded as u128\n287\t    pub d_used_nodes: *const usize,\n288\t    pub rules_len: usize,\n289\t    pub used_nodes_len: usize,\n290\t    pub lambda_len: usize,\n291\t    pub buffer_size: u32,\n292\t    pub d_intermediates: *mut F,\n293\t    pub buffer_stride: u32,\n294\t    pub num_x: u32,\n295\t    pub height: u32,\n296\t    pub g_shift: F,\n297\t}\n298\t\n299\t#[repr(C)]\n300\tpub struct Round0LogupCtx {\n301\t    pub selectors_cube: *const F,\n302\t    pub preprocessed: *const F,\n303\t    pub main_parts: *const *const F,\n304\t    pub eq_cube: *const EF,\n305\t    pub public_values: *const F,\n306\t    pub d_rules: *const u128,\n307\t    pub rules_len: usize,\n308\t    pub buffer_size: u32,\n309\t    pub d_intermediates: *mut F,\n310\t    pub buffer_stride: u32,\n311\t    pub numer_weights: *const EF,\n312\t    pub denom_weights: *const EF,\n313\t    pub denom_sum_init: EF,\n314\t    pub num_x: u32,\n315\t    pub height: u32,\n316\t    pub g_shift: F,\n317\t}\n318\t```\n319\t\n320\tAdd FFI extern declarations and safe wrapper functions for the batched launchers.\n321\t\n322\t## Invariants\n323\t\n324\t1. **Correctness**: The batched kernel must produce identical results to the per-AIR kernel for every AIR. The computation per block is identical to one block of the existing coset-parallel kernel. The `buffer_stride` per-AIR context field ensures each AIR's blocks index the intermediates buffer without collision.\n325\t2. **Memory safety**: All device pointers in context structs must be valid for the duration of the kernel launch. The shared intermediates buffer must be sized to hold all AIRs simultaneously within a sub-group. Weight buffers for logup must outlive the kernel launch.\n326\t3. **No regression at APC 0**: Large AIRs (lockstep mode) use the existing per-AIR path. The batched path only handles small AIRs. At APC 0 with ~20 AIRs/segment, most are large → minimal batching → no regression.\n327\t4. **Numerical equivalence**: The batched kernel uses GLOBAL intermediates mode only (no shared memory optimization). For most AIRs at APC 300, buffer_size is small enough that GLOBAL mode was already used. Verify via prove+verify correctness test.\n328\t5. **Keygen compatibility**: The new `Round0InteractionRules` field in `AirDataGpu` is computed at keygen. Existing serialized proving keys are not affected (this field is not serialized; it's computed in-memory).\n329\t6. **Identity coset**: The batched logup kernel handles coset_idx=0 as the identity coset with `g_coset = Fp::one()` and `skip_ntt = true`, matching the existing coset-parallel logup kernel behavior (logup_round0.cu lines 361-436).\n330\t7. **Uniform reduction**: Within each `(num_cosets)` group, all AIRs share the same reduction dimension. For zerocheck: `d = num_cosets * skip_domain` (FpExt). For logup: `d = 2 * num_cosets * skip_domain` (FracExt stacked as FpExt[2], via reinterpret_cast). The existing `batched_final_reduce_block_sums` works for both cases without modification.\n331\t8. **Intermediates sizing**: All `_zerocheck_r0_intermediates_buffer_size` and `_logup_r0_intermediates_buffer_size` calls in the batched path use `max_temp_bytes = usize::MAX` to prevent the internal `eval_config.cuh` launch-params logic from reducing `grid.x`. This ensures `buffer_stride = num_cosets * num_x_blocks * block_x` is consistent between the intermediates allocation size and the kernel indexing.\n332\t\n333\t## Measurement Plan\n334\t\n335\t1. Run `run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300} before and after the change.\n336\t2. Analyze with `spec.py` to compare STARK excl trace and Round 0 sub-metrics.\n337\t3. Run nsys profiling for APC 300 to verify kernel launch count reduction (~1500 → ~12-20).\n338\t4. Verify prove+verify passes for all 3 APC configs.\n339\t\n340\tExpected results (conservative):\n341\t- Round 0 APC 300: 294ms → 140-180ms (1.6-2.1x improvement)\n342\t- Round 0 APC 0: 177ms → ≤ 177ms (no regression; large AIRs use existing path)\n343\t- STARK excl trace APC 300: 1409ms → 1225-1275ms (130-180ms improvement)\n344\t\n345\tNew descriptor overhead (per group: ~290 CPU struct builds, ~10KB H2D upload, ~1ms compute) is minor compared to eliminated overhead (~1500 kernel launches, ~2000 buffer allocations, ~310 CPU DAG constructions).\n346\t\n347\t## Rollback Criteria\n348\t\n349\t1. Round 0 at APC 300 improves by less than 60ms (< 20% of current 294ms)\n350\t2. STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 30ms\n351\t3. Prove+verify fails for any APC configuration\n352\t4. GPU OOM error during batched kernel launch\n353\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01DJZrRxfovuAPecnesbZeka","session_id":"f2fb024d-582e-4612-82c1-d7c12af7ab35","uuid":"96c75104-ec99-4dca-bc7a-64460a4444f7","timestamp":"2026-04-13T13:06:28.461Z"}
